# The annotations of the game image: the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations. One line an entry:
#
#   @module NAME
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment    a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL

@module main
$A600 c Start ; the entry from N1: the IM 2 table, the start-up, the menu
$C4FD c MenuInterrupt ; the interrupt handler of the menu
$D566 c PlayInterrupt ; the interrupt handler of play, one a frame
$D59C c StartInterrupt ; the interrupt handler while the start-up runs
$AF11 c MoveShip ; the ship's move from Input: X by right / left, Y by down / up, each refused by CheckShipMove
$B052 c CheckShipMove ; carry: the ship may not stand at HL (X), A (Y)
$D37C c WaitKey ; ReadControls; while key 1 (Input bit 7) is held, the pause: wait for another key
$D38A c ReadControls ; Input from the redefinable keys (KeyTable) or the joystick the menu chose (Controls)
$D3CC b KeyTable 3 ; the keys, eight of 3 bytes: the port high byte, the bit mask, the key code (shown by the redefine menu)
$D3E4 Controls ; the menu's choice: 1 keyboard, 2 Kempston, 3 Interface 2
$A720 c RobotMenu ; landing on a robot of ours: DIRECT CONTROL, GIVE ORDERS, COMBAT MODE, LEAVE ROBOT; the order to +$0B
$ACD7 c ListMenu ; a list menu: HL the attribute of item 0 (an item every 3 rows), E the items + 1, A the first highlighted; down / up move, fire returns A
$AD57 c WaitRelease ; wait until no control is held
$C849 c ConstructionMenu ; landing on the warbase pad at altitude 15: choose the parts (MenuCursor, MenuParts), START ROBOT or EXIT MENU
$CAF0 b PartCosts 8 ; the parts' cost (bipod, tracks, anti-grav, cannon, missiles, phasers, nuclear, electronics), then the resource each takes first (an index into Resources)
$CDD8 c FindRobot ; Z: IY the robot record standing on map cell HL, B = 48 - its slot (slots 0-23 the player's)
$CDF5 c FindBuilding ; Z: IY the building record (Buildings) on map cell HL
$D42D c PrintInline ; print the text after the CALL: 0 ends, 1 Y X a position, 2 A an attribute, 3 the next line, 4 N a setting, else a character
@inline $D42D

@inline $C40C

# ---- r1 ----
# r1: $A600-$AFFF, start-up, the play loop, the robot menu and its submenus, the list menu, the game turn, the ship's move.
# Evidence: code reading, work/deep/l1.json (36046 frames, 3414 game turns), and experiments on an own instance (port
# base 20000): advance 10 miles, combat mode cannon, direct control, robot freed under the open menu, both game ends,
# the 24-robot pad bug. Entries already in game.ann (Start, RobotMenu, ListMenu, WaitRelease, MoveShip) get inner
# comments only; their better titles are in r1.md.

# --- Start ($A600, in game.ann): inner points
$A600 ; no interrupts while the vector table is built
$A601 ; SP = 0: the stack grows down from $FFFF
$A604 ; the IM 2 table: 257 bytes of $FD at $FE00-$FF00
$A60A FillVectorTable ; 256 bytes, then the 257th below
$A60F ; JP at $FDFD: every vector lands on Handler
$A614 ; StartInterrupt (EI; RET) while the menu runs
$A621 ; the random seed $FD00-$FD03 = $39,$30,$39,$30 (12345 twice)
$A62A ; the title menu: Z = START GAME, NZ = a saved game was loaded

$A62D c NewGameSetup ; New game: clear the robots, variables, buildings and shots, place the ship, build the map
> Entered from Start with the flags of the title menu MainMenu: NZ (option 5 loaded a saved game, which brings the robots, the map, the buildings, the shots and $FD00-$FDFB from tape) skips straight to EnterPlay; Z (option 0, START GAME) sets a new game up.
> Zeroes the robot records $DA00-$DCFF (48 x 16), the variables $FD04-$FDFC (everything past the random seed, below the JP of the IM 2 vector), the building table $FF01-$FFC9 and the five shot records $D7D3-$D7FF (5 x 9). Then: MapView = $DD00, ViewX = 0, ShipX = $11, ShipY = 10, Altitude = 0, the auto-climb counter $FD30 = 3 (the ship rises to 6 on its own at the start), the general resource of both sides = 20 (Resources and $FD4A). BuildMap then clears the map $DD00-$FCFF and the radar buffer $D800 and builds the landscape, the warbases and the factories. Falls into EnterPlay.
$A62D ; NZ: a loaded game, nothing to set up
$A62F ; the 48 robot records
$A63C ; the variables past the seed
$A649 ; the 56 building records (and 33 bytes more)
$A656 ; the 5 shot records
$A663 ; the view starts at the map's left edge
$A66F ; the ship over column 17, row 10, on the ground
$A67E ; auto-climb for 3 turns
$A683 ; 20 general resource for each side
$A68B ; build the map

$A68E c EnterPlay ; Draw the play screen and switch to the play interrupt
> DrawViewBackground and DrawPlayScreen build the play screen (the view, its frame, the radar, the status panel); then Handler = PlayInterrupt, which counts the frames in $FD34 and draws the radar each frame. Falls into PlayLoop. Entered from NewGameSetup, from a loaded game, and again after a game save (key 5, SaveGame) at $A71D.
$A694 ; PlayInterrupt from now on

$A69A c PlayLoop ; The play loop: one game turn a pass (at least 10 frames)
> One pass: WaitKey (the controls into Input; key 1 pauses here), MoveShip, WorldTurn (every robot record and the five shots), DrawView (draw the view), GameTurn (the random step, the clock, captures, the end of the game; waits until 10 frames have passed since the previous turn). The recording ran it 3332 times in 36046 frames: about 10.5 frames a turn, 5 turns a second.
> Then it looks under the ship (ShipCell: HL = the ship's map cell). Map bit 6 means something stands there. A robot of ours (FindRobot, B = 48 - slot >= 25) with the ship resting exactly on its top (Altitude = +$09 + +$0D) opens RobotMenu. Otherwise a building of type 0 (the pad of our warbase) with Altitude = 15 (the pad's top, the ship cannot sink below it) opens ConstructionMenu, but only when the warbase's door cell 4 rows below the pad and the three cells below the door (row +5, columns -1, 0, +1) are free of bit 6: the new robot is placed on the door (ShipY + 4, $CB6A). After the menu the new record gets +$05 = 4, +$06 = 5, +$0B = 0 (stop), +$0C = 100 (strength), +$0F = 1 (moves at once); CountRobots recounts the robots and UpdateStatusPanel reprints the panel.
> Bug (seen on the own instance): ConstructionMenu returns at once when all 24 of our slots are taken, with IY left on $DB80, the enemy's slot 24. The writes at $A6F3 then hit that enemy robot: its strength goes back to 100 and its order to 0, every turn the ship sits on the pad.
> Last, the game keys: Input bit 6 (key I in the default KeyTable) abandons the game (JP Start), bit 5 (key 5) saves the game to tape (SaveGame) and redraws the play screen (EnterPlay).
$A69A ; the controls; the pause while key 1 is held
$A69D ; the ship (unless a robot is under direct control)
$A6A0 ; the robots and the shots
$A6A3 ; the view
$A6A6 ; the turn: clock, captures, game end, frame pacing
$A6A9 ; HL = the map cell under the ship
$A6AC ; bit 6: a robot or a building under the ship
$A6B0 ; one of ours? B = 48 - slot
$A6B6 ; slots 24-47 (B < 25) are the enemy's
$A6BA ; the ship must rest exactly on the robot's top
$A6C3 ; landed on our robot
$A6C8 CheckWarbasePad ; not a robot: a building?
$A6CD ; type 0: the pad of our warbase
$A6D3 ; the pad's top is 15
$A6DA ; HL = the pad's cell + 4 rows: the door, where the robot appears
$A6E4 ; the door, the cell below it and its two neighbours
$A6EC ; any of them taken: no menu
$A6F0 ; IY = the free robot slot it filled (or $DB80 when none was free)
$A6F3 ; the new robot's start state
$A6FB ; order 0: stop and defend
$A6FF ; strength 100 %
$A703 ; +$0F = 1: its first step at once
$A707 ; recount the robots of both sides
$A70A ; reprint the status values
$A70D CheckGameKeys ; Input bit 6 (key I): abandon the game
$A715 ; Input bit 5 (key 5): save the game
$A71A ; "PRESS RECORD AND PLAY", the tape save
$A71D ; redraw the play screen

# --- RobotMenu ($A720, in game.ann): inner points
$A720 ; IX = the robot record (from FindRobot)
$A724 ; +$0A = 1: the player sits on it; the robot stops (UpdateRobot skips it)
$A728 ; MenuCursor = 4: LEAVE ROBOT highlighted first
$A72D ; $FD39 = 1: the panel shows the menu, UpdateStatusPanel keeps off it

$A732 c RobotMenuShow ; Robot menu: draw the four items, the current order and the strength, then choose
> Clears the side panel (ClearPanel) and prints DIRECT CONTROL, GIVE ORDERS, COMBAT MODE, LEAVE ROBOT at (4,23), every 3 rows, attribute $4D, and the title -ORDERS- at (17,23). Falls into ShowOrder, ShowOrderMiles and RobotMenuChoose. Every submenu comes back here when it is done ($A93E, $AA09, $AACC, $ABFD, the combat menu at $AC8B).
$A732 ; clear the side panel
$A735 ; the four items and -ORDERS-
$A738 t RobotMenuText ; The robot menu: DIRECT CONTROL, GIVE ORDERS, COMBAT MODE, LEAVE ROBOT, -ORDERS-
> A PrintInline string: (4,23) attribute $4D, each item two lines of 8 with an empty line between (code 3 = the next line, same column), then (17,23) attribute $46 "-ORDERS-".

$A795 c ShowOrder ; Robot menu: print the name of the robot's order
> Copies the 27-byte name of order +$0B from OrderNames ($A848 + 27 x order) into OrderLine, the string printed right after the CALL at $A7AA: three lines at (18,23), attribute $45.
$A795 ; B = order + 1
$A799 ; OrderNames - 27
$A79F OrderNameIndex ; HL += 27, order + 1 times
$A7A2 ; into the string after the next CALL
$A7AA ; print the order's name
$A7AD t OrderLineHead ; The order line: (18,23), attribute $45, then the copied name
$A7B2 s OrderLine ; The order's name, 27 bytes copied from OrderNames (zero in the image)
> Filled by ShowOrder before each print; the copied name brings its own end byte 0.

$A7CD c ShowOrderMiles ; Robot menu: for ADVANCE and RETREAT, the miles under the order's name
> Orders 1 and 2 only: prints at (19,25) the distance +$0E / 2 with two digits (PrintNumber2), between "ADVANCE" and "MILES" of the order's name. The recordings never gave these orders, so the printing code after the string ($A7DC) is still data in game.ctl; on the own instance an ADVANCE of 10 miles showed "10" there.
$A7CD ; order 1 or 2?
$A7D5 ; the position for the number
$A7D8 t MilesPos ; (19,25), the position of the miles
$A7DC c ShowMilesCode ; Code the recordings never ran: LD A,(IX+$0E); SRL A; CALL $D3E5
> The miles, stored doubled in +$0E, printed with two digits and a leading space. Runs for orders 1 and 2 (seen on the own instance), then falls into RobotMenuChoose.

$A7E4 c RobotMenuChoose ; Robot menu: choose an item and run it; LEAVE ROBOT closes the menu
> Prints the strength, runs ListMenu on the four items (HL = $5837, the attribute of row 1 column 23, E = 5, A = MenuCursor) and keeps the choice in MenuCursor, so the menu reopens on it. 1 DIRECT CONTROL ($A93B), 2 GIVE ORDERS (OrdersMenu), 3 COMBAT MODE (CombatMenu $AC00), 4 LEAVE ROBOT: a beep, +$06 = 0, +$0A = 0 (the robot carries on with its order), the auto-climb $FD30 = 5 turns so the ship rises off the robot and the menu does not reopen, then CloseRobotMenu.
> CloseRobotMenu ($A812) clears the panel, sets $FD39 = 0 and redraws the status panel (ShowStatusPanel); ListMenu also jumps there when the robot is destroyed while its menu is open (seen: freeing +$01 under the open menu closed it).
$A7E4 ; the strength
$A7E7 ; item 1 at row 4, column 23
$A7EA ; four items
$A7EC ; highlight the last choice
$A7F5 ; 1: DIRECT CONTROL
$A7F9 ; 2: GIVE ORDERS
$A7FD ; 3: COMBAT MODE
$A801 LeaveRobot ; 4: LEAVE ROBOT
$A806 ; +$06 = 0
$A80A ; +$0A = 0: no longer held by the player
$A80D ; climb for 5 turns
$A812 CloseRobotMenu ; clear the panel and give it back to the status display
$A819 ; the status panel

$A81D c ShowStrength ; Robot menus: print STRENGTH and the robot's strength in %
> Prints "STRENGTH" at (22,23) and the value of +$0C at (23,25), three digits with leading spaces and "%" (a robot is born with 100). A negative +$0C (a dying robot, UpdateRobot counts it up) shows as 0 % (poked $80: "0%"). Called once a turn by every robot menu loop. IX = the robot record.
$A81D ; "STRENGTH", then the position of the value
$A820 t StrengthText ; "STRENGTH" at (22,23) attribute $46, then (23,25) attribute $47
$A833 c ShowStrengthValue ; Print the strength +$0C as "nnn%"
> The tail of ShowStrength after its inline string: a negative strength is shown as 0; PrintNumber3 prints HL with three digits, E = $20 makes the leading zeros spaces; the "%" goes out through PrintCharKeep.
$A836 ; negative: a dying robot
$A83B ; HL = the strength
$A83E ; leading zeros as spaces
$A843 ; "%"

$A848 t OrderNames ; The names of the orders, 27 bytes each (order 0: STOP AND DEFEND)
> Nine records of 27 bytes, indexed by +$0B: three lines of 8 joined by code 3, ended by 0. ShowOrder copies one into OrderLine. Order 0 is "  STOP  ", "  AND   ", " DEFEND ".
$A863 t OrderName1 ; Order 1: ADVANCE (the miles are printed into the blank middle line)
$A87E t OrderName2 ; Order 2: RETREAT (the miles in the middle line)
$A899 t OrderName3 ; Order 3: DESTROY ENEMY ROBOTS
$A8B4 t OrderName4 ; Order 4: DESTROY ENEMY FACTORYS
$A8CF t OrderName5 ; Order 5: DESTROY ENEMY WARBASES
$A8EA t OrderName6 ; Order 6: CAPTURE NEUTRAL FACTORYS
$A905 t OrderName7 ; Order 7: CAPTURE ENEMY FACTORYS
$A920 t OrderName8 ; Order 8: CAPTURE ENEMY WARBASES

$A93B c DirectControlItem ; Robot menu item 1: direct control, then the robot menu again
> Not run in the recordings; run on the own instance.

$A941 c DirectControl ; Drive the robot with the controls until fire
> +$0A = 2 and +$05 = 0, then a loop of game turns without the ship's own move: WorldTurn moves the robot from Input ($B184 -> $B450) and calls MoveShip from there ($B4A8), so the ship travels on the robot; DrawView draws the view, MenuRadarTick blinks the ship on the radar, GameTurn, ShowStrength. Fire gives the robot back (+$0A = 1, a beep) and returns. Returns at once (Z) when the robot is destroyed (+$01 = 0).
> Used by the robot menu (item 1) and by the combat menu (MOVE ROBOT, $AC93). Not run in the recordings. On the own instance: +$0A went to 2, holding right turned the robot (+$08 4 -> 1) and moved it one column, the ship's ShipX followed (22 -> 23); fire set +$0A back to 1.
$A941 ; +$0A = 2: the robot follows Input
$A949 ; the fire that chose the item must go first
$A94C DirectControlLoop ; a game turn with the robot driven
$A952 ; the ship's radar blink
$A95B ; destroyed: back
$A960 ; the controls
$A963 ; fire: give the robot back
$A967 ; +$0A = 1: held, not driven

$A971 c OrdersMenu ; GIVE ORDERS: the five orders
> Clears the panel and prints SELECT ORDERS at (4,24) and the five items from (7,23): STOP AND DEFEND, ADVANCE ?? MILES, RETREAT ?? MILES, SEARCH & DESTROY, SEARCH & CAPTURE. Falls into OrdersMenuChoose.
$A977 t OrdersMenuText ; GIVE ORDERS: SELECT ORDERS and the five items

$A9EC c OrdersMenuChoose ; GIVE ORDERS: choose; stop at once, advance / retreat ask the miles, search asks the target
> ListMenu at HL = $5897 (row 4: item 1 at row 7), E = 6, A = 1. Item 1 sets order 0 (stop and defend) and returns to the robot menu. Items 2 and 3 set order 1 (advance) or 2 (retreat) and go on to the distance menu (DistanceMenu, $AA19 on). Items 4 and 5 go to TargetMenu with the flags of CP 3 (Z = SEARCH & DESTROY).
$A9F6 ; the item, 1-5
$A9FA ; the fire must go
$A9FE ; 1: STOP AND DEFEND
$AA01 ; order 0
$AA0C OrderMove ; 2 ADVANCE (A = 1), 3 RETREAT (A = 2); 4, 5 the searches
$AA11 ; order 1 or 2
$AA15 ; clear the panel for the distance menu
$AA1C ; ADVANCE: its title, then the distance menu
$AA1F t AdvanceTitle ; "ADVANCE " at (5,23), attribute $4F
$AA2D c AdvanceJump ; Code the recordings never ran: JR $AA40, past the RETREAT title

$AA2F c RetreatTitle ; GIVE ORDERS, RETREAT: the title of the distance menu
> Prints "RETREAT " at (5,23), attribute $4F. The bytes after the string ($AA40-$AACE) are the distance menu, code that no recording ran and game.ctl still holds as data (next entries).
$AA32 t RetreatTitleText ; "RETREAT " at (5,23), attribute $4F
$AA40 c DistanceMenu ; Code the recordings never ran: CALL PrintInline, the distance menu's texts
> The distance menu of ADVANCE and RETREAT (run on the own instance: up 3 times, down once, fire = 10 miles, +$0E = 20, +$0B = 1). The texts: "?? MILES" under the title, SELECT DISTANCE at (9,23), "0 MILES" at (13,24).
$AA43 t DistanceText ; The distance menu: ?? MILES, SELECT DISTANCE, 0 MILES
$AA6E c DistanceSelect ; Code the recordings never ran: choose the miles, 0-50 by 5
> D = the miles, from 0. A loop of game turns (ShowStrength, WorldTurn, DrawView, MenuRadarTick, GameTurn; a destroyed robot closes the menu, JP $A812). Up adds 5, down takes 5 (the value must stay 0-50: CP $33), each change reprinted at (13,23) attribute $46 with a beep $14.
> Fire ($AAB8): +$0E = miles x 2; 0 miles turns the order back into 0 (stop); a beep, WaitRelease, back to the robot menu. Layout: $AA6E LD D,0; $AA70 the turn; $AA88 WaitKey, BIT 4; $AA8F the step: (Input >> 2) AND 3, down 1 -> -5, up 2 -> +5; $AAA4 PrintInline (13,23) $46; $AAAD the number (PrintNumber2); $AAB8 the end.

$AACF c TargetMenu ; SEARCH & DESTROY or SEARCH & CAPTURE: the three targets
> Z (the destroy item): clears the panel and prints SEARCH & DESTROY, SELECT TARGET and ENEMY ROBOTS / ENEMY FACTORYS / ENEMY WARBASES; NZ goes to CaptureMenu.
$AACF ; SEARCH & CAPTURE
$AAD8 t DestroyMenuText ; SEARCH & DESTROY: SELECT TARGET, ENEMY ROBOTS, FACTORYS, WARBASES
$AB3D c DestroyMenuChoose ; SEARCH & DESTROY: choose the target, orders 3-5
> ListMenu at HL = $5937 (item 1 at row 12), E = 4, A = 1; the item + 2 is the order (3 robots, 4 factories, 5 warbases) for SetOrder.
$AB4A ; order 3-5

$AB4E c CaptureMenu ; SEARCH & CAPTURE: the three targets
> Prints SEARCH & CAPTURE, SELECT TARGET and NEUTRAL FACTORYS / ENEMY FACTORYS / ENEMY WARBASES.
$AB54 t CaptureMenuText ; SEARCH & CAPTURE: SELECT TARGET, NEUTRAL FACTORYS, ENEMY FACTORYS, ENEMY WARBASES
$ABB9 c CaptureMenuChoose ; SEARCH & CAPTURE: choose the target, orders 6-8
> The same list as DestroyMenuChoose; the item + 5 is the order (6 neutral factories, 7 enemy factories, 8 enemy warbases).
$ABC6 ; order 6-8

$ABC8 c SetOrder ; Store a search order, look for its first target, or fall back to stop
> A = the order (3-8) into +$0B. Orders 4 and 5 (destroy factories, warbases) need the nuclear bomb (+$07 bit 6); without it the order becomes 0. Otherwise $FD51 = 0 (our side), +$0E = $FF and FindTargetBuilding looks for a target of the order from the robot's X (+$02/+$03): D, the index it found, goes to +$0E; no target (Z) makes the order 0. A beep, WaitRelease, back to the robot menu. Ran 7 times in the recording (destroy robots, capture neutral and enemy factories).
> CombatMenu ($AC00) follows in the same block: the robot menu's item 3.
$ABCB ; orders 4 and 5 need the bomb
$ABD9 FindOrderTarget ; HL = the robot's X
$ABE1 ; $FD51 = 0: the search runs for our side
$ABE5 ; no target yet
$ABE9 ; Z: nothing to go for
$ABEC ; the target found
$ABF1 OrderRefused ; order 0: stop and defend
$ABF5 OrderSet ; the beep of a choice
$AC00 CombatMenu ; COMBAT MODE: the six items, then the code at $AC7C
$AC06 t CombatMenuText ; COMBAT MODE: NUCLEAR BOMB, FIRE PHASERS, FIRE MISSILES, FIRE CANNON, MOVE ROBOT, STOP COMBAT
> Printed at (4,23), attribute $4D, an item every 3 rows like the robot menu.
$AC7C c CombatMenuCode ; Code the recordings never ran: the combat menu
> No recording entered COMBAT MODE, so these 91 bytes are still data. ListMenu on the six items (HL = $5837, E = 7), STOP COMBAT highlighted first ($AC7F A = 6); the strength printed first.
> 6 STOP COMBAT: back to the robot menu. 5 MOVE ROBOT: DirectControl, then the menu again on that item. 1-4 need the weapon: +$07 rotated left item + 1 times leaves in carry bit 6 (nuclear), 5 (phasers), 4 (missiles) or 3 (cannon); without it a low buzz ($FA) and the menu again.
> 1 NUCLEAR BOMB: IY = IX, NuclearBlast (the blast), then the menu closes (JP $A812). 2-4: shot record 0 at $D7D3; if it is free (+$01 = 0) A = 5 - item (3 phasers, 2 missiles, 1 cannon) and FireShot launches it from the robot along its facing (+$08), then a view redraw and a game turn; a busy shot slot buzzes.
> Seen on the own instance: FIRE CANNON on a bipod + cannon robot wrote +$05 = 4 (the robot's facing), +$06 = 5, +$07 = 1, +$08 = $0A into $D7D3; FIRE MISSILES without missiles changed nothing. Layout: $AC7C the strength; $AC81 the list; $AC93 MOVE ROBOT; $AC99 the weapon test; $ACA9 the bomb; $ACB4 the shot slot; $ACCE the buzz.

# --- ListMenu ($ACD7, in game.ann): inner points
$ACD7 ; the fire or move that opened the menu must go first
$ACDC ; D = the highlighted item
$ACDD ; $4E: highlighted
$ACE2 ListMenuLoop ; the robot destroyed (+$01 = 0)?
$ACE8 ; drop our return: close the robot menu
$ACEC ListMenuKey ; the controls
$ACF5 ; fire: chosen
$ACF9 ; bit 2 down / bit 3 up, now bits 1 / 2
$ACFE ; A = 2 for down, 0 for up
$AD00 ; item - 1, then + 2 for down
$AD02 ; item 0 does not exist
$AD04 ; past the last item
$AD09 ; $4D: the old item back to normal
$AD10 ; highlight the new one
$AD15 ; the click of a move
$AD1A ListMenuTurn ; a game turn while the menu waits
$AD28 ; the strength
$AD2F ListMenuChosen ; $4F: the chosen item
$AD35 ; the beep of a choice
$AD3A ; A = the item

$AD3C c PaintMenuItem ; Colour list item A: 2 rows of 8 attributes with C
> HL = the attribute of item 0; item A starts $60 bytes (3 rows) further per item. Fills 8 attributes, moves down a row ($18 more) and fills 8 more with C ($4D normal, $4E highlighted, $4F chosen). A must be 1 or more. Keeps DE and HL.
$AD3F ; 3 attribute rows an item
$AD45 ; the item's top row
$AD4A ; the next row, same column
$AD4F ; its bottom row

# --- WaitRelease ($AD57, in game.ann)
$AD59 WaitReleaseLoop ; until Input is 0

$AD62 c GameTurn ; One game turn: the random step, the clock, the daily production, the clock print
> Called once a pass by PlayLoop and by every robot menu loop (3414 times in the recording). Random steps the random generator $FD00-$FD03. Clock ($FD35 minutes, $FD36 hours, $FD37-$FD38 days) gains 5 minutes a turn: 60 minutes make an hour, 24 hours a day, and a new day runs DailyProduction. Then the clock print: days at (0,27), five digits with leading spaces; "HH.MM" on the next line. Goes on in GameTurnEnd.
$AD62 ; the random generator
$AD65 ; 5 minutes a turn
$AD70 ; an hour
$AD79 ; a day
$AD82 ; the day's production of both sides
$AD85 ; position, setting 0, attribute $57
$AD88 t ClockPos ; The clock: (0,27), setting 0, attribute $57

$AD90 c GameTurnEnd ; Game turn, second half: the clock print, the game end, captures, the frame pacing
> Prints days (PrintNumber5) and, a line down (PrintNewline), hours "." minutes (PrintNumber2Zero, two digits with zeros). CheckGameEnd.
> Captures: the 28 capture points at $FD70 (5 bytes each: X 2 bytes, Y, owner and type, count; points 0-3 the warbases, 4-27 the factories) are checked each turn. While map bit 6 is set on a point's cell (a robot stands on the building's door) its count goes up; when it reaches 144 ($90) FindRobot takes the robot there and its side (+$0A bit 7) captures the building: GiveWarbase for a warbase, GiveFactory (point - 4) for a factory. An empty door resets the count. The recording shows every capture after a count of 121-143 at the 250-frame samples. Any robot captures, whatever its order. After a capture CountBases recounts the buildings and UpdateStatusPanel reprints the status.
> Last, the pacing: waits until PlayInterrupt has counted 10 frames in $FD34 since the last turn, then clears it. A turn is at least 10 frames: an hour 120, a game day 2880 (about 58 s).
$AD93 ; days, five digits
$AD96 ; the next line
$AD99 ; hours, two digits with zeros
$AD9F ; "."
$ADA4 ; minutes
$ADAA ; won or lost?
$ADAF ; the capture points
$ADB3 ; 28: 4 warbases, 24 factories
$ADB5 ; C = captures this turn
$ADB7 CaptureLoop ; the point's door cell
$ADC3 ; a robot on it?
$ADC7 ; empty: the count starts again
$ADCD CaptureCount ; one more turn on the door
$ADD3 ; 144 turns: taken
$ADDE ; the robot standing there
$ADE1 ; E = its side: 0 ours, 1 the enemy's
$ADEA ; no robot found
$ADEF ; one more capture
$ADF1 ; A = the point's index
$ADF4 ; 0-3: a warbase
$ADF9 ; the warbase changes side
$ADFE ; 4-27: factory A - 4
$AE05 CaptureNext ; 5 bytes a point
$AE13 ; any capture?
$AE17 ; recount the buildings of both sides
$AE1A ; reprint the status values
$AE1D TurnPace ; at least 10 frames a turn
$AE24 ; the next turn's frames

$AE29 c UnusedTapeCode ; Unreferenced code: keys 6 and 7 together jump into the tape ROM
> 15 bytes of code nothing calls or jumps to: LD A,$EF; IN A,($FE); AND $18; RET NZ (unless 6 and 7 are both held); LD HL,$A600; PUSH HL; DI; JP $04B0. $04B0 is inside an instruction of the 48K ROM's SAVE code; with Start as the return address it looks like a debugging leftover (a memory dump to tape). Never runs.

$AE38 c DailyProduction ; A new day: each side's buildings add to its resources
> Our side from $FD3A into Resources, the enemy from $FD42 into $FD4A (ProduceSide), then the panel reprint (UpdateStatusPanel). Ran 11 times in the recording, once a game day.
$AE38 ; ours: counts $FD3A
$AE41 ; the enemy's: counts $FD42, resources $FD4A

$AE4E c ProduceSide ; One side's production: 5 general a warbase, 2 a factory of each kind
> DE = the side's building counts (warbases, then the factories of electronics, nuclear, phasers, missiles, cannon, chassis), HL = its 7 resources (general first, then the same order). General += 5 x warbases; each other resource += 2 x the factories of its kind; every resource stops at 99. In the recording the general resource grew by 5 a day with one warbase, electronics by 2 with one factory.
$AE4E ; 5 x warbases
$AE53 ; into the general resource
$AE56 ; six factory kinds
$AE5A ; 2 x the factories of the kind

$AE62 c AddCapped ; (HL) += A, at most 99
$AE69 ; store

$AE6B c CheckGameEnd ; The end of the game: the enemy has no warbase (won) or we have none (lost)
> $FD42 (the enemy's warbases) = 0: GameWon. Else $FD3A (ours) = 0: GameStop, "YOU HAVE NO BASES LEFT / BETTER LUCK NEXT TIME!" at (22,0), then the common end at $AEEA. Both tested by poking the counts on the own instance (the messages showed, the handler became StartInterrupt, fire went back to the title menu).
$AE6B ; the enemy's warbases
$AE71 ; ours
$AE76 ; lost
$AE7C t LostText ; "YOU HAVE NO BASES LEFT", "BETTER LUCK NEXT TIME!" at (22,0), attribute $45
$AEAF c LostJump ; Code the recordings never ran: JR $AEEA, the common end

$AEB1 c GameWon ; The enemy has no warbase left: "INSIGNIANS DESTROYED / YOU HAVE WON !"
> Stops play (GameStop), prints the message at (22,0) and falls into the common end at $AEEA: a low buzz, 100 frames, WaitRelease, then the first control (Input not 0, the chosen keys or stick only) goes back to Start and the title menu.
$AEB7 t WonText ; " INSIGNIANS DESTROYED ", "    YOU HAVE WON !    " at (22,0), attribute $45
$AEEA c GameOverWait ; Code the recordings never ran: the end of a won or lost game
> LD A,$FA; CALL $CCAC (the buzz); LD B,100; HALT; DJNZ (2 s); CALL WaitRelease; CALL WaitKey until a control is held; JP Start.

$AF00 c GameStop ; Stop play: give the panel back to the status and switch to the idle interrupt
> $FD39 = 0, clears the panel, prints the status panel (ShowStatusPanel), Handler = StartInterrupt (no frame count, no radar). Used by both ends of the game.

