$AFE6 c ScrollRadar ; Move the radar window by 64 columns when the ship nears its edge
> The radar shows 128 map columns: from column $FD1B x 8 ($FD1C = the same as a column, 16 bits). With A = ShipX / 8 - $FD1B: below 2 (the ship within 16 columns of the left edge) the window moves 8 units left, unless it is at 0; 14 and more moves it 8 units right, at most to $30 (columns 384-511). A move sets $FD52 = 1: the radar bitmap is rebuilt from the map by BuildRadar at the ship's next mark ($B029). Called by MoveShip every turn (3332 times).
$AFE9 ; C = the window's left edge, in units of 8 columns
$AFED ; A = ShipX / 8
$AFF5 ; the ship's place in the window, 0-15
$AFFF ; left by 8 units


# ---- r2 ----
# r2: $B000-$B7FF, the radar window and the ship's dot, the height probes, the world turn, the robot AI
# (orders, steering, sight, fire), the shots and their damage.
# Evidence: code reading, work/deep L1 (call counts, read/write ranges), the deep TTD (shot and robot records around
# frames 18680-18850), experiments on an own instance (port base 20600): direct control, ADVANCE 5 MILES, the
# radar window scroll, a breakpoint at $B2B8 after a factory's owner was poked.

# --- the tail of the radar window follow (the routine starts at $AFE6, outside this range)
$B000 ; the window would move 8 columns left
$B002 ; already at the left end: stay
$B005 RadarScrollRight ; the ship is 14 or more units right of the window's left edge: 8 units right
$B008 ; $38 would pass the map's end: stay at $30 (columns $180-$1FF)
$B00B RadarSetWindow ; RadarLeft ($FD1B) = A (in 8-column units)
$B00E ; RadarColumn ($FD1C) = A * 8, the first map column the radar shows
$B017 ; the radar must be rebuilt (PutShipOnMap does it)

$B01D c LiftShipFromMap ; clear the ship's occupant bit in its map cell, toggle its radar dot
> Called by MoveShip first thing each game turn, before the ship moves: the cell under the ship (ShipCell: $DD00 + $200 * ShipY + ShipX) loses bit 7, the occupant bit robots test (FreeDirections) when the ship hangs lower than their top, and the ship's dot on the radar is toggled off when it is on (BlinkShipDot). PutShipOnMap undoes both after the move.
$B01D ; HL = the ship's map cell
$B020 ; the ship is no longer in this cell
$B022 ; and the dot goes (when the tick is odd)
$B024 c PutShipOnMap ; set the ship's occupant bit in its new map cell, rebuild the radar if the window moved, toggle the ship's dot
> Called at the end of MoveShip (and by BuildMap). Sets bit 7 of the ship's cell, then goes on into RefreshRadar. Recording: 3333 calls, once a game turn.
$B024 ; HL = the ship's map cell
$B027 ; the ship occupies it
$B029 c RefreshRadar ; rebuild the radar when RadarDirty is set, then blink the ship's dot
> RadarDirty ($FD52) is set when the window moved ($B017) and by other code that changes the map (the start, a loaded game). BuildRadar then redraws both radar layers $D800 / $D900 from the map (a pixel for every cell of type $0F or more, the high ground) and plots every live robot's dot; the flag is cleared. Falls into BlinkShipDot. Also reached from MenuRadarTick.
$B02F ; terrain bitmap of the 128-column window and all robots' dots
$B032 ; done until the window moves again
$B036 c BlinkShipDot ; on odd ticks toggle the ship's 2x2 dot on the radar's player layer
> RadarBlink ($FD1E) counts the game turns (MoveShip increments it between LiftShipFromMap and PutShipOnMap, MenuRadarTick in the menus). When it is odd, ToggleRadarDot XORs a 2x2 dot at (ShipX, ShipY) into the layer B = 0 ($D800, shown with attribute $4D by PlayInterrupt in alternate frames). Off-window positions draw nothing (RadarDotAddress). The pairs of toggles around each move leave the dot on every other turn: the ship's dot blinks.
$B03B ; even tick: no change
$B043 ; B = 0: the player's layer $D800
$B045 ; XOR the dot, return from there
$B048 c MenuRadarTick ; one blink step of the ship's dot while a menu is open
> Called by the robot menu loop (DirectControl) and ListMenu each pass, so the world goes on and the ship's dot keeps blinking while the player sits in a menu: toggles the dot (BlinkShipDot), advances RadarBlink, rebuilds the radar if needed and toggles again on the new tick (RefreshRadar). 83 calls in the deep recording.
$B04E ; the next tick
$B04F ; rebuild if dirty, toggle on the new tick
$B052 ; keep X for MoveShip
$B053 ; HL = the cell (X, Y)
$B057 ; C = the highest top found so far
$B059 ; the 2x2 footprint, terrain and objects: (X, Y)
$B05D ; (X+1, Y)
$B062 ; (X+1, Y-1)
$B066 ; (X, Y-1)
$B06A ; objects only, the anchors whose footprint overlaps ours: (X-1, Y-1)
$B06F ; (X-1, Y)
$B074 ; the row Y+1 only inside the map
$B079 ; (X-1, Y+1), (X, Y+1), (X+1, Y+1)
$B084 ; carry when the ship is lower than the highest top: C stays for MoveShip's sinking
$B08A c TerrainTop ; C = max(C, the ground height of the terrain type at DE)
> DE a map cell. The cell's type (bits 0-4) indexes the ground height table TerrainHeights $D7BC (00 00 02 02 02 02 03 03 06 06 06 06 00 00 00 07 0F 07 0F ...: types 2-5 height 2, 6-7 (the wreck a destroyed robot leaves) 3, 8-$0B 6, $0F/$11 7, $10/$12 15). Keeps the higher of it and C (KeepHigher). Used by CheckShipMove (through CellTop) and GroundUnderFootprint; 39396 calls.
$B08D ; TerrainHeights
$B094 ; keep the higher one
$B096 c CellTop ; C = max(C, the ground and the object on cell DE)
> TerrainTop, then falls into ObjectTop: the highest point of a cell, used for the 2x2 footprint in CheckShipMove.
$B099 c ObjectTop ; C = max(C, the top of a robot or a building anchored at cell DE)
> When the cell has bit 6 (an object anchored there): a robot (FindRobot) counts with its body height +$09 plus the ground under it +$0D; else a building (FindBuilding) with its height from BuildingTops by type. A cell with bit 6 that is neither (a shot) counts nothing. B and C are kept.
$B09B ; no object anchored here
$B09F ; a robot on this cell?
$B0A5 ; its top: the body height plus the ground it stands on
$B0AB c KeepHigher ; C = max(C, A)
> The common tail of TerrainTop and ObjectTop.
$B0B0 ObjectTopBuilding ; not a robot: a building?
$B0B4 ; neither: a shot, nothing to count
$B0B5 ; the building's type
$B0C1 b BuildingTops 9 ; The heights of the building types 0-8 for the ship and the robots
> Read by ObjectTop with the type byte of the Buildings record: type 0 (the warbase pad) $0F, the factory types 1-6 $16, $15, $15, $16, $16, $16, types 7 and 8 $19. The pad's 15 is the altitude the ship sinks to on it, where ConstructionMenu opens.

$B0CA c WorldTurn ; one game turn of the world: the enemy commander, every robot, every shot
> Called once a game turn by the play loop (PlayLoop) and by the menus (DirectControl, ListMenu), so robots fight and walk on while the player chooses: 3415 calls in 36046 frames, a turn is about 10 frames. First EnemyCommander (one enemy slot built or re-ordered), then UpdateRobot for each of the 48 robot records $DA00 whose +$01 is not 0, then UpdateShot for each of the 5 shot records of 9 bytes at $D7D3 whose +$01 is not 0. Robots and shots are taken in slot order, the player's slots 0-23 first. Shot record 0 ($D7D3) is the one the player fires from the robot menu's COMBAT MODE (DirectControl -> FireShot); the robots use records 1-4 (FindFreeShot).
$B0CD ; the 48 robot records
$B0D3 WorldTurnRobotLoop ; a live robot (+$01 = the high byte of its cell, 0 = free)
$B0E3 ; the 5 shot records
$B0E9 WorldTurnShotLoop ; a live shot
$B0FA c UpdateRobot ; one robot's game turn: dying, destroyed or a step of its AI
> IY = the robot record. Strength +$0C decides: positive, the robot acts (RobotStep); negative, the robot is dying: +$0C counts up from $FC (set by MoveShot's hit) and the object bit 6 of its cell is toggled each turn (cleared on even values, set on odd), so for four turns other robots and shots see it only every other turn; 0, the robot is destroyed (RobotDestroyed). 49165 calls.
$B0FE ; strength 0: destroyed
$B100 ; strength positive: alive
$B103 ; dying: one turn closer to 0
$B10C ; the old value's bit 0: the object bit flickers
$B113 RobotDyingShow ; odd: the object is there
$B116 RobotDestroyed ; remove the robot: a wreck on bare ground, the dot, the slot, the counts
$B11C ; no object on its cell any more
$B11E ; OR of the four footprint cells (X, Y), (X+1, Y), (X+1, Y-1), (X, Y-1)
$B128 ; bits 0-5: anything but bare type-0 ground?
$B12C ; bare ground: a wreck, terrain type 6 or 7 at random
$B133 ; PutBlock: a 2x2 terrain feature of type A at HL (the anchor A, the other three cells A + $20)
$B136 RobotRemove ; erase its radar dot: layer B = +$0A bit 7 (0 ours, 1 the enemy's)
$B149 ; the slot is free
$B14D ; recount: OurRobots $FD41, EnemyRobots $FD49
$B150 ; and the status panel
$B154 c RobotStep ; a live robot: wait for its step delay, then look, fire, steer and move one step
> Entered from UpdateRobot with strength > 0 (JP P). A robot the player sits on (+$0A = 1, the robot menu is open) does nothing. Side ($FD51) = 0 ours / 1 the enemy's (+$0A bit 7), read by the target searches. The step delay +$0F counts the turns down; at 0 the robot leaves its cell (bit 6 cleared, radar dot erased) and FreeDirections gives C, the directions it can step to (bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, the Input order).
> Direct control (+$0A = 2): the stick decides (RobotDirectControl). Otherwise SpotEnemies gives E, the directions with a live opponent in sight. An opponent ahead (E AND the facing +$08): the robot moves on if it can and fires when a shot record of its side is free and its weapons allow (FindFreeShot, FireShot; a shot stops it for this step). An opponent to a side: it turns to it (+$05 = that direction, one picked at random among several, +$06 = 2). No opponent: it keeps its direction +$05 for +$06 more steps while that direction stays free, else it takes a new course for 3-6 steps from SteerByOrder (RobotNewCourse). RobotMove then turns or steps, reloads the delay (SetStepDelay) and puts the robot back on the map (PlaceRobot: the record's x/y/cell, bit 6, the dot).
> Recording: 9652 steps; the shot records and strengths at frames 18680-18850 match FireShot and MoveShot (robot 2 hit twice by an enemy missile, 100 -> 70 -> 40; robot 25 hit by our cannon, 100 -> 80 -> 60 -> 40).
$B157 ; the player sits on it: frozen while the robot menu is open
$B15A ; Side: 0 ours, 1 the enemy's
$B160 ; the step delay
$B164 ; time to step: off the map
$B16D ; erase its radar dot
$B181 ; C = the free directions from this cell
$B187 ; under direct control?
$B18D ; E = the directions with an opponent in sight
$B193 ; nobody in sight
$B195 ; an opponent straight ahead?
$B19A ; move on ahead if free (else stand)
$B1A2 ; IX = the shooter, IY = the free shot record
$B1A9 ; both of our side's shots are flying
$B1AB ; each weapon (bits 3 cannon, 4 missiles, 5 phasers) fires this turn at even odds
$B1B3 ; no weapon chosen this turn
$B1B5 ; the highest chosen weapon: phasers 3, missiles 2, cannon 1
$B1B7 RobotWeaponPick ; count the rotations until the weapon bit leaves
$B1BC ; launch the shot
$B1C3 ; the shot costs the step: stand, decide again next step
$B1CD RobotNoShot ; no shot: walk on ahead
$B1D7 RobotTurnToEnemy ; opponents to a side: face one of them
$B1D9 ; several: one at random
$B1E9 RobotNoEnemy ; nobody in sight: keep the course while it lasts and is free
$B1F5 RobotNewCourse ; a new course for 3-6 steps
$B1FF ; at least one free direction: ask the order
$B205 ; the robot blew itself up (a nuclear charge, NuclearBlast)
$B20D RobotMove ; turn or step in +$05, the new delay, back on the map
$B21F ; PlaceRobot: store x / y / the cell, set bit 6, plot the dot

$B222 c SteerByOrder ; C = the direction the robot's order wants, chosen among the free ones in C
> IY the robot, C the free directions (FreeDirections); out C a single direction bit, or 0 to stand. B keeps the free set. By the order +$0B:
> 0 stop: C = 0. 1 advance / 2 retreat: right / left (RobotLeanTo, a 3/4 chance to take that direction when free, else a random free one other than the opposite); RobotStepPos counts the distance +$0E.
> 3 destroy robots: the target is the opponent robot of index +$0E (TargetRobotRecord). It is kept while it lives and is less than 50 columns away; else the nearest live opponent by column distance is taken (FindNearestOpponent), none: a random free direction.
> 4-8 the building orders: the target is record +$0E of the factory table $FD84 or the warbase table $FD70 (TargetBuildingTable). It is kept while its owner bits are still the ones the order looks for; else the nearest such building nobody of the side is already after is taken (FindTargetBuilding). None: the robot stands, and an enemy robot falls back to destroy robots.
> The aim (RobotAim): D = the directions toward the target, horizontal from the sign of the column difference, vertical from the row difference; for a building, while the columns differ, the row aimed at is 3 below the building's row. More than 7 columns away the robot leans horizontally like an advance (RobotLeanTo); closer, one of the free wanted directions at random, a horizontal one kept for |dx| - 1 steps (+$06). On the target (D = 0): the destroy orders 4-5 go to the nuclear charge at NuclearBlast (outside this range); the others turn the robot to face down and stand.
> Bug (experiment, breakpoint at $B2B8 after poking the target factory's owner): FindTargetBuilding leaves HL past the table, not on the new target, so the step right after a new building target aims at the bytes below $FDFF (the JP at $FDFD and Handler: row $66, column $C300): one step of a wrong course, corrected on the next decision.
$B223 ; the order
$B229 ; 0 stop: stand
$B22B ; 1-2: advance / retreat
$B230 ; 4-8: the building orders
$B232 ; 3 destroy robots: a target already?
$B239 ; HL = the target robot's record
$B23D ; dead (its +$01 = 0)?
$B24C ; HL = |its column - ours|
$B254 ; less than 50 columns: chase it
$B258 SteerNewRobotTarget ; find the nearest live opponent
$B267 ; +$0E = its index, $FF none
$B26A ; nobody: wander
$B26D SteerAtRobot ; DE = the target's column, B = our row - its row
$B282 ; HL = our column - its column
$B289 SteerBuildingOrder ; HL = the owner byte of the first record, E = the owner the order wants
$B28C ; HL = the target record's owner byte (5 bytes a record)
$B299 ; still a building we want?
$B29C ; no: find another
$B2AA ; found one
$B2AC ; none left: stand
$B2AE ; ours: stand and wait
$B2B3 ; the enemy's: hunt robots instead
$B2B8 ; the new target (HL is not on it: the bug above)
$B2BB SteerAtBuilding ; B = our row - the building's row, DE = its column
$B2CC ; HL = our column - its column
$B2D1 ; columns differ: aim at the row 3 below it
$B2D5 RobotAim ; horizontal: 2 left when we are right of it, 1 right when left
$B2DC RobotAimVertical ; vertical: 8 up when we are below it, 4 down when above
$B2E8 ; D = the wanted directions
$B2EC ; on the target: stand
$B2F4 ; destroy factories / warbases: the nuclear charge
$B2F9 ; already facing down: stand
$B2FF ; else turn down and stay so
$B306 ; L = |the column difference| (low byte)
$B30E ; 8 or more columns: lean horizontally
$B314 ; the wanted directions that are free
$B315 ; none: any free direction
$B318 ; only vertical ones: pick one
$B31C ; the columns agree: pick one
$B320 ; keep a horizontal course for |dx| - 1 steps
$B326 RobotLeanTo ; D = the horizontal part (1 right, 2 left), E = all but the opposite one
$B32D ; wanted direction blocked: any free one
$B330 ; C = the wanted direction
$B331 ; taken with a 3/4 chance (two random bits)
$B33B ; else: among the free ones but the opposite
$B33E c PickOneDirection ; C = one direction bit picked at random from the set in B
> Masks C with random bytes until exactly one bit is left (CountDirections); when nothing is left it starts again from B. B must not be 0. The common exit of SteerByOrder's branches and used by RobotTurnToEnemy for several opponents.
$B33F PickOneLoop ; C AND a random byte
$B347 ; exactly one: done
$B348 ; still several: narrow on
$B34A ; none left: from the whole set again
$B34D c FindTargetBuilding ; D = the nearest building the order wants and no teammate is after; Z when none
> A = the order (3 goes to FindNearestOpponent), HL = the robot's column, IY the robot, Side set. The order picks the table and owner (TargetBuildingTable); every record with those owner bits is offered to ConsiderBuilding, which skips it when a robot of our side with the same order already has it as its +$0E, and keeps the nearest by column distance (KeepNearest). Out D = the record's index (or $FF), A = D + 1 (Z: none). HL is left past the table: the cause of the bug noted at SteerByOrder. Recording: 112 calls (also from the enemy's order choice at $B989 and SetOrder).
$B353 ; A' = the order, for ConsiderBuilding
$B355 ; the search: DE' = our column, BC' = the best distance ($2000), C = the index, D = $FF
$B358 ; HL = the first record's owner byte, B = the records, E = the owner wanted
$B35B FindTargetLoop ; owner bits 5-7 the ones wanted?
$B362 ; next record (L only: the tables sit inside page $FD)
$B369 ; A = the index + 1: Z when none was found
$B36C c ConsiderBuilding ; offer building C to KeepNearest unless a teammate with the same order already targets it
> HL = the record's owner byte (+$03), C its index, A' the order. Scans the 24 records of our side ($DA00 ours, $DB80 the enemy's by Side) for a live robot whose order +$0B is A' and whose target +$0E is C. None: the record's column (+$00/+$01) goes to KeepNearest. The robot that asks is in the list too; its +$0E holds its old target (or $FF).
$B371 ; E = the order
$B373 ; our side's robots
$B383 ConsiderBuildingLoop ; a live robot ...
$B389 ; ... with the same order ...
$B38F ; ... after the same building?
$B3A2 ; nobody: HL = the building's column
$B3AE ConsiderBuildingTaken ; taken: skip it
$B3B3 c KeepNearest ; keep candidate C when the column HL is nearer to DE' than the best BC'
> The comparison step of the nearest searches (InitNearestSearch sets it up): HL' = |HL - DE'|, and when it is below BC' it becomes the best and D = C. Only the column distance counts; rows are ignored.
$B3B6 ; the distance
$B3C3 ; nearer: the new best, D = its index
$B3CA c ColumnDistance ; HL = |HL - DE|
> A 16-bit absolute difference (the sign of the 16-bit result decides); A = 0 on the negative path.
$B3CE ; negative: 0 - HL
$B3D5 c TargetBuildingTable ; HL, B, E = the building table, its size and the owner a building order wants
> A = the order. Orders 5 and 8 (destroy / capture warbases): HL = $FD73, the owner byte of the first of the 4 warbase records (Warbases $FD70, 5 bytes each), B = 4; the others: HL = $FD87, the owner byte of the first of 24 factory records (Factories $FD84), B = 24. E = the owner bits sought: $20 (the computer's) for our robots, $40 (ours) for the enemy's (Side = 1), 0 (neutral) for order 6, capture neutral factories.
$B3E2 ; warbases
$B3E7 ; the opponent's mark: $20 for us ...
$B3F0 ; ... $40 for the enemy
$B3F6 ; capture neutral: owner bits 0
$B3F9 c TargetRobotRecord ; HL = the opponent robot record of index +$0E
> HL = $DB80 + 16 * (+$0E) for our robots, $DA00 + 16 * (+$0E) for the enemy's (Side). Used by SteerByOrder's destroy-robots branch.
$B3FC ; the enemy's robots for us, ours for the enemy
$B411 c InitNearestSearch ; start a nearest-by-column search from column HL
> DE' = HL (our column), BC' = $2000 (no candidate yet), C = 0 (the first index), D = $FF (the result: none). Used by FindTargetBuilding and FindNearestOpponent with KeepNearest.
$B41D c FindNearestOpponent ; D = the index of the nearest live opponent robot by column; Z when none
> HL = our column, IY the robot, Side set. Runs KeepNearest over the 24 opponent records ($DB80 the enemy's for us, $DA00 ours for the enemy) whose +$01 is not 0 (a dying robot counts). Out D = the slot within the side (or $FF), A = D + 1 (Z: none). 426 calls.
$B422 ; the opponent's records
$B432 FindOpponentLoop ; a live record: compare its column
$B44D ; A = the index + 1: Z when none
$B450 RobotDirectControl ; direct control: the stick steers the robot
> Reached from RobotStep for +$0A = 2 (not run in the deep recording; experiment below).
$B451 ; Input, with the pause on key 1
$B455 ; B = the free directions
$B456 ; pushing the way it faces: go only if free (a turn needs no free cell)
$B45D ; exactly one direction?
$B462 ; none or several: go on the way it went, if still free
$B46B ; Input = the step, so MoveShip carries the ship along (TurnOrStep)
$B471 c TurnOrStep ; turn the robot toward direction A, or step one cell when it faces it
> IY the robot, HL its column, B its row, A a direction bit (0: nothing). Facing +$08 not A: the robot turns, never by 180 degrees in one go (right <-> left goes through down / up, down <-> up through right / left), and +$06 is incremented so the turn does not shorten the course. Facing A: StepPosition moves HL/B, GroundUnderFootprint sets +$0D, and under direct control (+$0A = 2) MoveShip moves the ship by the same Input and the ship sits on the robot's top (Altitude = +$09 + +$0D).
> Experiment (DIRECT CONTROL on a new bipod + cannon robot at (22,14)): holding right the robot turned from facing down (4) to right (1) in one step and moved one column a step (6 turns, about 60 frames); after the release it went on one more step in the same direction; the ship moved with it and Altitude stayed 17 (bipod 11 + cannon 6, ground 0).
$B473 ; already facing it: step
$B478 ; C = the new facing
$B47C ; right <-> left: turn down / up first
$B486 ; down <-> up: turn right / left first
$B48E ; face it; the turn does not count as a course step
$B495 TurnOrStepStep ; step: the new column / row in HL / B
$B498 ; the highest ground under the new footprint
$B49E ; direct control?
$B4A8 ; the ship moves by the same Input
$B4AF ; and sits on the robot's top
$B4B9 c StepPosition ; move HL (column) / B (row) one cell in direction A; advance / retreat count their distance
> A bit 0 right (HL+1), 1 left (HL-1), 2 down (B+1), 3 up (B-1). For the orders 1 advance and 2 retreat (not under direct control) +$0E is the distance still to go: a column in the order's direction (right for advance, left for retreat) takes one off and at 0 the order becomes 0 stop (+$06 = 0); a column the other way adds one, up to $63.
> Experiment: ADVANCE with 5 MILES (the robot menu stores miles * 2 into +$0E = 10): the robot went 4 columns right (+$0E 10 -> 6), 5 rows up round an obstacle (+$0E unchanged), 6 more columns right and then stood with order 0 and +$0E = 0.
$B4C7 ; not right
$B4D5 ; not left: down?
$B4DA ; up?
$B4DE OrderDistanceUp ; a column away from the order's aim: one more to go (max 99)
$B4F2 OrderDistanceDown ; a column toward it: one less; there: stop
$B4FC ; arrived: the order becomes stop
$B505 c CountDirections ; flags of (the number of set bits in C's low nibble) - 1
> Z: exactly one direction; carry: none; neither: two or more. Counts bits 0-3 of C, keeps BC.
$B509 CountDirectionsLoop ; add each bit
$B513 c FreeDirections ; C = the directions the robot at cell HL may step to
> IY the robot, HL its anchor cell. A robot covers 2x2 cells: its anchor (X, Y) carries the object bit 6, plus (X+1, Y), (X, Y-1), (X+1, Y-1). D = the first terrain type it cannot enter, by its locomotion: bipod 8 (types 0-7, ground heights 0-3), tracks $0C (also 8-$0B, height 6), anti-grav $0F (also $0C-$0E, height 0 in TerrainHeights); the types from $0F (heights 7 and 15) stop all. E = the bits that block: 6 (objects) always, 7 (the ship's cell) when the ship hangs lower than the robot's top. One probe a direction (CanMoveRight, CanMoveLeft, CanMoveDown, CanMoveUp); bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up. 9652 calls.
$B515 ; the ship lower than our top?
$B51E ; then bit 7 (the ship's cell) blocks too
$B520 ; objects always block
$B526 ; bipod: types below 8
$B52B ; tracks: types below $0C
$B530 ; anti-grav: types below $0F
$B532 ; right free?
$B53B ; left free?
$B544 ; down free?
$B54D ; up free?
$B557 c CanMoveRight ; Z when the robot at HL can step right
> Terrain and objects at the column it enters, (X+2, Y-1) and (X+2, Y); an object anchored at (X+2, Y+1) (its footprint would overlap) when that row exists.
$B567 ; the row below only inside the map
$B56F c CanMoveLeft ; Z when the robot at HL can step left
> Terrain and objects at (X-1, Y-1) and (X-1, Y); objects anchored at (X-2, Y-1), (X-2, Y) and (X-2, Y+1).
$B587 ; the row below only inside the map
$B58F c CanMoveDown ; Z when the robot at HL can step down
> Blocked at the bottom row; terrain and objects at (X, Y+1) and (X+1, Y+1); objects anchored at (X+1, Y+2), (X, Y+2) and (X-1, Y+2) when that row exists.
$B591 ; the bottom row: blocked
$B5A1 ; the row below only inside the map
$B5B1 c CanMoveUp ; Z when the robot at HL can step up
> Blocked when Y-2 is above the map (the footprint already covers Y-1); an object anchored at (X-1, Y-2), terrain and objects at (X, Y-2) and (X+1, Y-2).
$B5B5 ; Y-2 off the map: blocked
$B5C8 c CellOffMapFree ; Z: a probe that runs past the bottom row finds the way free
> The shared exit of the probes for rows below the map.
$B5CA c CellBlocked ; NZ: the way is blocked
> The shared exit for an unpassable terrain type or a map edge.
$B5CD c TestCell ; Z when cell HL is passable: type below D and none of the bits E
> 67380 calls.
$B5D3 ; an object (or the low ship) in it?
$B5D6 c GroundUnderFootprint ; A = the highest ground under the 2x2 footprint anchored at (HL, B)
> HL a column, B a row. TerrainTop over (X, Y), (X+1, Y), (X+1, Y-1), (X, Y-1); HL and BC are kept. The robots' +$0D (the ground they stand on, added to their height for the ship) and the shots' wall test (MoveShot).
$B5F3 c SetStepDelay ; +$0F = the turns until the robot's next step, by the ground and its locomotion
> The ground class from +$0D: 0 flat, 1-3 low, 4 and more high; the column from the lowest set part bit of +$07: 0 bipod, 1 tracks, 2 anti-grav. StepDelays gives the turns. The recorded records agree (robot 0, a bipod on flat ground, counts 6 down).
$B5FD ; low ground
$B603 ; high ground
$B60A SetStepDelayPart ; D = the locomotion: the index of the first set part bit
$B61D b StepDelays 3 ; Game turns a robot step takes: rows flat, low, high ground; columns bipod, tracks, anti-grav
> 6 4 3 / 8 6 3 / 9 7 4. Read by SetStepDelay; a game turn is about 10 frames, so a bipod on flat ground steps every 60 frames.
$B626 c SpotEnemies ; E = the directions in which a live opponent robot is in sight
> IY the robot. For each direction (up, down, left, right: E's bits 3, 2, 1, 0 as in Input) ScanLane looks from the robot's anchor up to 8 cells; in the facing direction +$08 the range is 10, 12 with electronics (+$07 bit 7), and three parallel lanes are scanned (the columns X-1, X, X+1 for up / down, the rows Y-1, Y, Y+1 for left / right). 9652 calls.
