$B69D ; same side: no
$B6A3 ; dead or dying: no
$B6A7 ; an opponent in sight
$B6AD ScanLaneDone ; back with E
$B6B0 w LaneSteps 4 ; The map steps of the four directions: right +1, left -1, down +$200, up -$200
> Read by ScanLane (from LD HL,$B6AE and two INC HL a bit of D), in the Input bit order.
$B6B8 c FindFreeShot ; IY = a free shot record of the shooter IX's side; Z when one is free
> Each side has two shot records of 9 bytes: ours $D7DC and $D7E5, the enemy's $D7EE and $D7F7 (by IX +$0A bit 7). Z with IY on the first one whose +$01 is 0; NZ when both fly. Record 0 ($D7D3) is not handed out here: it is the player's COMBAT MODE shot, launched from the robot menu (DirectControl, not in the recording).
$B6C2 ; the enemy's records
$B6CC ; the second record
$B6D6 c FireShot ; launch shot IY of weapon A from robot IX in its facing direction
> Called by RobotStep and by COMBAT MODE in the robot menu (DirectControl, IX = IY = the robot, record 0). Shot record (9 bytes): +$00/+$01 the map cell, +$02/+$03 the column, +$04 the row, +$05 the direction, +$06 the steps left, +$07 the weapon (1 cannon, 2 missiles, 3 phasers), +$08 the ground height that stops it ($0A). The range is 5 steps (7 for missiles), one more with electronics; each step goes 2 cells. The first step is taken at once (MoveShot); when the shot survives it, SoundFx ($FD53) = 1, the firing sound. Recording: an enemy missile at frame 18680, D7EE: 7B F3 7B 00 0B 08 06 02 0A (column $7B, row 11, up, 6 steps left after the first turn, missiles).
$B6DB ; missiles fly further
$B6E1 ; electronics: one step more
$B6EB ; the robot's facing
$B6F1 ; ground of height 10 or more stops it (TerrainHeights 15)
$B6FE ; the first step
$B707 ; it flies: the firing sound
$B70D c UpdateShot ; one game turn of shot IY: off its cell, count its range, a step
> Clears the object bit of the shot's cell, counts +$06 down (0: the shot is gone, ShotGone) and goes on into MoveShot from its column / row. 90 calls in the deep recording.
$B713 ; the shot leaves its cell
$B715 ; out of range: gone
$B724 c MoveShot ; move shot IY 2 cells, then hit a wall, an object or fly on
> HL the column, B the row. Off the rows 0-15, or ground of height +$08 ($0A) or more under its 2x2 footprint (only the types $10 / $12, height 15; heights up to 7 let it pass): gone. Then the 3x3 cells around the new cell are tested for an object (bit 6); none: the shot marks its own cell with bit 6 and flies on. An object that is a live robot (either side: friendly fire is possible) takes damage ((60 - its top) / 4) * (weapon + 1), the top being +$09 + +$0D, so tall robots on high ground take less: strength 0 or below makes it $FC (dying, UpdateRobot) with SoundFx $C8, else SoundFx $FA. Any other object (a building, another shot, a dying robot) just ends the shot (SoundFx $FC). The recorded hits agree: a missile (2) into a 19 high robot takes 10 * 3 = 30, a cannon shell 10 * 2 = 20.
$B727 ; right: 2 columns
$B72E ; left
$B735 ; down: 2 rows
$B73C ; up
$B741 ; off the map's rows: gone
$B747 MoveShotStore ; the new position
$B750 ; the ground under it
$B753 ; too high: it hits the wall
$B75A ; the new cell
$B763 ; the row above: Y-1, columns X-1 to X+1 (when inside the map)
$B77C MoveShotRow ; the shot's row
$B78E ; the row below (when inside the map)
$B7A3 MoveShotFlies ; nothing near: the shot occupies its cell
$B7A7 MoveShotHit ; hit: E = the weapon, C = the sound for a miss
$B7AE ; a robot anchored at the cell hit?
$B7B3 ; alive?
$B7BA ; (60 - its top) / 4 ...
$B7C7 ; ... times the weapon + 1
$B7C8 MoveShotDamage ; one more multiple
$B7CC ; the hit sound
$B7D1 ; the strength left
$B7D7 ; destroyed: dying for 4 turns, the explosion sound
$B7DE MoveShotGone ; the shot is gone
$B7EA ShotGone ; out of range or off the map: the shot is gone with the short sound

$B7F4 c EnemyCommander ; once a game turn the computer looks at one of its 24 robot slots: builds a robot in a free one or re-thinks a robot's order
> Called first by WorldTurn. A random number 0-31 picks the slot; 24-31 (a quarter of the turns) do nothing. Side = 1. The slot's record is $DB80 + 16 * n. A live robot goes to EnemyReplan (outside this range): one with order 0 at once, any other with a 1/32 chance a turn, gets a new order (a nuclear robot destroy factories or warbases, the others one of the capture orders; destroy robots when the order finds no target). A free slot tries a build at a random enemy warbase (owner $20 in the Warbases table): random parts (exactly one locomotion, at least one and not all four of the weapon / nuclear parts, at least 1, 2 or 3 of those parts while the computer has 0-7, 8-15 or 16-23 robots, EnemyRobots $FD49), paid from EnemyResources ($FD4A, through MenuResources and PartCosts like the player's), the shortfall of the other resources taken from the general one, at most half of it (at least 11); the robot is set up facing down, order stop, strength $64, +$0A = $80, height +$09 the sum of the part heights $D7B4 (bipod 11, tracks 7, anti-grav 8, cannon 6, missiles 6, phasers 7, nuclear 7, electronics 7). 3415 calls.
$B7F4 ; a random slot ...
$B7F9 ; ... of the computer's 24, or none this turn
$B7FC ; * 16: the record's offset from $DB80


# ---- r3 ----
# r3: $B800-$C3FF. The enemy's robot factory and order planner (the second half of $B7F4), the nuclear blast,
# the bases (owners, counts, buildings), the map builder and its scenery / shape tables, the main menu, load / save,
# redefine keys. Needs `@inline $C40C` (outside this range): PrintKeyPrompt ends with JP PrintInline, so the key names
# after each CALL $C40C are text, not code (the t entries $C382-$C3EE below).

# --- the rest of $B7F4 (the entry is r2's): one enemy slot a game turn
