$BA87 c NuclearFlash ; The nuclear blast's flash: the view's paper flickers through random colours with a fading rumble
> Called only by the blast ($BA76). Saves the 20 x 20 attributes of the view (from $5821, row 1 column 1) to $5B00-$5C8F, then 61 times: a burst of noise ($BAEE, 244 samples first, 4 fewer and a longer delay each time) and a new random paper colour (2-7, never the previous one) on every attribute of the view whose paper is not black. Restores the saved attributes at the end. The noise takes about 3.3 s of beeper time in total; the game stands still meanwhile.
> Verified by an experiment (own instance, PC set to $B99F with a poked nuclear robot): the run passes here after the bases and robots are hit. $5B00 is free scratch at that time (the printer buffer and above, also the save checksum of $C2E8).
$BA87 ; copy the view's attributes to $5B00
$BA90 FlashSaveRow
$BA91 FlashSaveCell ; LDI counts BC down: INC C keeps B the column counter
$BA96 ; the next attribute row: 32 - 20
$BA9F ; B = 244 samples, C = 1: the noise's length and delay
$BAA2 FlashLoop ; one flash: noise, then the colours
$BAAC FlashPickPaper ; a random paper (bits 3-5) of colour 2 or more, not the previous one (E)
$BAB9 FlashRow
$BABA FlashCell ; repaint the paper of every attribute whose paper is not black
$BAC4 FlashNextCell
$BAD1 ; B fell by 3 in $BAEE and 1 here: 61 flashes
$BAD3 ; put the attributes back
$BADC RestoreRow
$BADD RestoreCell
$BAE9 RestoreNextRow

$BAEE c NuclearRumble ; One burst of the blast's noise: B samples of ROM bytes to the speaker, C the delay between
> Plays B samples: bit 4 (the speaker) of the ROM bytes from $0090 on, the border black, with a delay loop of C x 24 T-states after each. Returns B - 3 and C + 3, so the next burst of NuclearFlash is shorter and lower: a dying rumble. Only NuclearFlash calls it.
$BAEF ; the ROM bytes as noise
$BAF2 RumbleSample ; bit 4 to the speaker, border 0
$BAF9 RumbleDelay
$BB01 ; the next burst: 3 samples fewer, a longer delay

$BB09 c CountBases ; Count each side's bases: warbases and the factories of each type
> Clears PlayerBases ($FD3A, 7) and EnemyBases ($FD42, 7) and goes through the 28 base records ($FD70, 5 bytes): a base whose +3 has bit 6 counts for the player, bit 5 for the enemy, at index +3 bits 0-2 (0 warbases, 1-6 the factories of type 1-6: electronics, nuclear, phasers, missiles, cannon, chassis, the resource each makes); neutral and destroyed (bit 7) bases do not count. The status panel shows these counts ($D2C1); the daily production ($AE38) adds 5 general per warbase and 2 per factory to its resource; a side with no warbase loses ($AE6B).
> Called after every change of owner (the capture tick $AE17), after the blast ($BA81), and by the map builder ($BCCA). Ran 18 times in the deep recording.
$BB09 ; the player's counts
$BB0E ClearPlayerCounts
$BB13 ; skip PlayerRobots ($FD41)
$BB16 ClearEnemyCounts
$BB1B ; the +3 byte of base 0, 28 bases
$BB20 CountBaseLoop
$BB22 ; destroyed: not counted
$BB2C ; not the player's: the enemy's?
$BB31 ; neutral: not counted
$BB33 CountBaseType ; count[type] += 1
$BB39 CountNextBase ; the next record (all inside page $FD)

$BB40 c CountRobots ; PlayerRobots ($FD41) and EnemyRobots ($FD49): the live records of each side
> Counts the robot records whose +1 (the map cell's high byte) is not 0: slots 0-23 into PlayerRobots, slots 24-47 into EnemyRobots. The status panel shows them (the ROBOTS line); the enemy's factory ($B86F) builds better-armed robots as its count grows. Called after each birth and death of a robot.
$BB40 ; the +1 byte of slot 0

$BB50 c CountLiveRobots ; A = the live records among 24 robot records from HL (+1 not 0); HL moves past them
$BB57 CountLiveLoop
$BB5C CountLiveNext

$BB61 c GiveFactory ; Factory A (0-23) changes hands: to the player (B = 0) or the enemy (B = 1); its flag moves
> Sets the factory's owner bits ($BBB9), removes the flag buildings at both sides of its roof (the cells x-2 and x+2 of row y-2) and puts the new owner's flag: type 7 (the player's) at x-2, type 8 (the enemy's) at x+2. The factory's own building (type 1-6 at x, y-2) stays.
> Called by the capture tick ($AE01) when a robot has stood on the factory's door for 144 ticks. Ran 17 times in the deep recording (captures by both sides).
$BB61 ; the factories' +3 bytes
$BB67 ; HL = the roof row y-2, x+2
$BB6D ; no flag at x+2
$BB74 ; no flag at x-2
$BB79 ; the player's flag at x-2
$BB7B ; the enemy's at x+2
$BB7F GiveFactoryFlag ; type 7 or 8
$BB82 ; add the flag building

$BB86 c GiveWarbase ; Warbase A (0-3) changes hands: to the player (B = 0) or the enemy (B = 1)
> Sets the owner bits ($BBB9) and rebuilds the warbase's buildings on its pad row y-4: removes those at x-4, x and x+4, then for the player puts the landing pad (type 0, x) and the player's flag (type 7, x-4); for the enemy only its flag (type 8, x+4). So a captured warbase gives the player a second pad where the construction menu opens.
> Called by the map builder ($BCB3-$BCC7: warbase 0 the player's, 1-3 the enemy's) and by the capture tick ($ADF9). In the deep recording only the four set-up calls ran.
