$BBAC ; the player: the pad (type 0) at x
$BBB4 ; and the flag (type 7) at x-4

$BBB9 c SetBaseOwner ; Base record A (HL = the table's +3 bytes): owner = the player (B = 0) or the enemy; HL = its door cell
> HL is $FD73 (warbases) or $FD87 (factories). Replaces bits 5-7 of the base's +3 with $40 (the player) or $20 (the enemy), which also clears the destroyed bit 7. Falls into BaseCell: HL = the map cell of the base's (x, y), its door.
$BBC2 ; the player's bit 6 ...
$BBC6 ; ... or the enemy's bit 5
$BBC7 ; keep the type, set the owner
$BBCD BaseCell ; HL at a base's +3: HL = the map cell of its x (+0/+1), y (+2)

$BBD8 c DestroyFactory ; Factory A (0-23) destroyed by a nuclear blast: buildings removed, blocks turned to debris
> Marks the factory destroyed ($BC1C), removes its three buildings on the roof row y-2 (the flags at x+2 and x-2 and the factory at x) and draws the factory's shape (ShapeFactory) again in debris: every block a random tile 6 or 7. Verified by an experiment: factory 0 at (39,6) became $80, its building at (39,4) went and its blocks became tiles 6 / 7.
$BBDE ; keep the door cell
$BBDF ; the roof row y-2, x+2
$BBF2 ; the shape from the door
$BBF3 ; ShapeFactory

$BBF9 c DestroyWarbase ; Warbase A (0-3) destroyed by a nuclear blast: buildings removed, blocks turned to debris
> Marks the warbase destroyed ($BC1C), removes the buildings at x-4, x and x+4 of its pad row y-4 and redraws ShapeWarbase in debris (tiles 6 / 7). Verified by an experiment: warbase 1 at (261,8) became $80, its flag building at (265,4) went, the enemy's warbase count fell from 3 to 2 and its walls became debris.
$BBFF ; keep the door cell
$BC00 ; the pad row y-4, x
$BC16 ; ShapeWarbase

$BC1C c MarkBaseDestroyed ; Base A of the table HL (+3 bytes): +3 = $80 (destroyed, no owner); HL = its door cell
$BC23 ; destroyed: no owner, no type
$BC27 DrawRuins ; draw the shape DE at cell HL with every block a random debris tile (6 or 7)
$BC2B ; any tile: debris
$BC35 DrawRuinsStep ; the shape's next step: columns C, rows B; (0,0) ends
$BC3E ; HL to the next block

$BC43 c AddBuilding ; Add a building: map cell HL, type C (0 pad, 1-6 factory, 7 the player's flag, 8 the enemy's flag)
> Takes the first free record of Buildings (56 x 3 bytes: the cell, the type; a high byte 0 = free), writes the cell and the type, and sets bit 6 of the map cell. With all 56 records taken it does nothing. The buildings are what the ship stands on (their heights by type at $B0C1: pad 15, factories 21-22, flags 25) and where landing opens the construction menu (type 0).
$BC43 ; the high bytes of the cells
$BC48 AddBuildingFind
$BC52 AddBuildingStore ; the cell's bit 6: something to find here

$BC5D c RemoveBuilding ; Remove the building on map cell HL, if any: its record freed, bit 6 of the cell cleared
> Keeps IY and BC. Uses FindBuilding.

$BC6F c BuildMap ; A new game's map: clear it, place the scenery and the 28 bases, give the warbases their owners
> Called once by the new game set-up ($A68B). Clears the map $DD00-$FCFF and the radar bitmap $D800-$D8FF, places the scenery lists SceneryWest (columns 0-255) and SceneryEast (256-511), then the bases BaseListWest and BaseListEast (records into $FD70: 4 warbases, 24 factories, all neutral), gives warbase 0 to the player and warbases 1-3 to the enemy, counts the bases, marks the radar for a rebuild and puts the ship on the map ($B024).
> The resulting map: the player's warbase at (22,9), the enemy's at (261,8), (369,8), (494,8); 24 factories, four of each type; read back from the live instance after a new game.
$BC6F ; the map: 16 rows of 512 cells
$BC7C ; the radar bitmap
$BC89 ; the scenery, columns 0-255
$BC91 ; the scenery, columns 256-511
$BC99 ; the factories' records from $FD84, the warbases' from $FD70
$BCB1 ; warbase 0: the player's
$BCB6 ; warbases 1-3: the enemy's
$BCCD ; rebuild the radar map
$BCD2 ; the ship's cell bit 7, the ship's radar dot

$BCD6 c PlaceScenery ; Place a scenery list at HL (3-byte records: tile or shape, x low, y; 0 ends), D = x high byte
> A record with bit 7 clear is one 2 x 2 block of that tile ($BD91), with bit 7 set the shape of that index ($BD61). The cell is $DD00 + $200 * y + D * 256 + x.
$BCE1 ; HL = the cell: $DD00 + 2 * y pages + DE
$BCEA ; bit 7: a shape
$BCF2 PlaceSceneryShape
$BCF5 PlaceSceneryNext

$BCF9 c PlaceBases ; Place a bases list at HL (3-byte records: type, x low, y; $FF ends), D = x high byte
> Type 0: a warbase. Its record goes to IX (the warbases' table) and ShapeWarbase is drawn from the cell. Type 1-6: a factory of that type. Its record goes to IY (the factories' table), ShapeFactory is drawn and the factory's building (that type) is added two rows up, at (x, y-2). A record: +0/+1 x, +2 y, +3 the type (the owner bits come later), +4 0 (the capture counter).
$BD0E ; type 0: a warbase
$BD10 ; a factory's record
$BD2C ; shape 1, ShapeFactory
$BD33 ; the factory's building at (x, y-2), its type
$BD3E PlaceWarbase ; a warbase's record
$BD58 ; shape 0, ShapeWarbase

$BD61 c DrawShape ; Draw shape A (bit 7 ignored, the index into ShapeTable) from map cell HL
> A shape is a list of 3-byte steps: a tile (0 = none) drawn as a 2 x 2 block at the current cell, then the move to the next cell: columns (signed), rows (signed); a move of (0,0) ends after its tile. DrawRuins ($BC27) walks the same lists with debris tiles.
$BD6C DrawShapeStep
$BD6E ; a 2 x 2 block of the tile
$BD79 ; the move (0,0) ends the shape

$BD7F c StepCell ; HL += C columns (signed) and B rows (signed: H += 2 * B); DE kept
$BD8A StepCellAdd

$BD91 c PutBlock ; A 2 x 2 block of tile A with its bottom left at map cell HL
> Writes the cells (x, y), (x+1, y), (x, y-1), (x+1, y-1): the first with the tile as it is, the other three with bit 5 set (the other parts of a block; the drawing and the blast take only the cell with bit 5 clear). Keeps HL and DE; BC is lost. Used by the map builder, the ruins, the blast and a dead robot's wreck ($B133).
$BD93 ; 2 rows of 2 cells
$BD97 PutBlockRow
$BD99 PutBlockCell ; the first cell plain, the rest with bit 5
$BDA1 ; the row above

$BDA9 b SceneryWest 3 ; The scenery of columns 0-255: 69 records (tile or shape, x, y) and the 0 that ends them
> Read by BuildMap through PlaceScenery with D = 0. Bit 7 clear: a 2 x 2 block of that tile; bit 7 set: shape (byte & $7F) of ShapeTable. Tiles used: $02-$09 and $0D (low features), $11 and $12 (free-standing blocks, height 7 and 15: the ones a nuclear blast turns to debris). Shapes 2-10. The tile heights the ship and the robots meet are the table $D7BC.
$BE79 b SceneryEast 3 ; The scenery of columns 256-511: 68 records and the 0 that ends them
> Read by BuildMap through PlaceScenery with D = 1.
$BF46 b BaseListWest 3 ; The bases of columns 0-255: 13 records (type, x, y), $FF ends
> Type 0 a warbase, 1-6 a factory making resource 1-6 (electronics, nuclear, phasers, missiles, cannon, chassis). Here: the player's warbase at (22,9) and the factories (39,6) type 4, (53,3) 6, (62,10) 1, (79,3) 5, (97,3) 3, (125,3) 2, (140,11) 6, (160,5) 1, (180,3) 3, (217,3) 4, (227,9) 5, (241,3) 6. The (x, y) is the base's door: the cell a robot stands on to capture it (the capture tick $AD90) and where the enemy's new robots appear (warbases).
$BF6E b BaseListEast 3 ; The bases of columns 256-511: 15 records (type, x, y), $FF ends
> The enemy's three warbases at (261,8), (369,8), (494,8) (given to the enemy by BuildMap) and twelve factories. With BaseListWest: 4 warbases and 24 factories, four of each type, the order of the bases table $FD70.
$BF9C w ShapeTable 11 ; The shapes' addresses, index 0-10 (DrawShape)
> 0 the warbase, 1 the factory (both drawn by PlaceBases), 2-10 the scenery shapes (a scenery record $82-$8A).
$BFB2 b ShapeWarbase 3 ; Shape 0: the warbase, 15 blocks of tiles $0F (height 7) and $10 (height 15) around the door
> Steps (tile, columns, rows): a first move of (-4,-4), then blocks reaching 4 columns either side and up to 8 rows above the door. The pad, the block above the door at (x, y-4), is a tile $10 block: altitude 15 over it is the landing height of ConstructionMenu.
$BFE2 b ShapeFactory 3 ; Shape 1: the factory, tiles $0F at (x-2, y) and (x+2, y), $10 on the roof row y-2 at x-2, x, x+2
> The door (x, y) stays free between the two low blocks.
$BFF4 b ShapeLowTiles 3 ; Shape 2: 4 x 3 blocks of tiles $02-$05 (height 2)
$C018 b ShapeMidTiles 3 ; Shape 3: 4 x 3 blocks of tiles $08-$0B (height 6)
$C03C b ShapeTallWallV 3 ; Shape 4: four blocks of tile $12 (height 15) in a column
$C048 b ShapeTallWallH 3 ; Shape 5: four blocks of tile $12 in a row
$C054 b ShapeEdgeFence 3 ; Shape 6: four blocks of tile $15 (height 99, impassable) in a column: the fences at both ends of the map
> Placed at (12,1), (12,9), (503,1), (503,9): the row of posts at the map's left and right edges.
$C060 b ShapeFlatLineV 3 ; Shape 7: tiles $0C, $0D, $0D, $0E (height 0) in a column
$C06C b ShapeFlatLineH 3 ; Shape 8: tiles $0C, $0D, $0D, $0E in a row
$C078 b ShapeLowWallH 3 ; Shape 9: four blocks of tile $11 (height 7) in a row
$C084 b ShapeMixedWallH 3 ; Shape 10: tiles $11, $12, $11 in a row
$C08D s MapTablesSpare ; Unused: zero to $C0FF

# --- the main menu
$C100 c MainMenu ; The main menu: clear the screen, the title, the options; Z = start a new game, NZ = a game was loaded
> Called by Start ($A62A); Start goes on to the new game set-up on Z and straight to play on NZ ($A62D). Prints the title (PrintTitle) and the options 0-5; the current controls (Controls, $D3E4) show in cyan, the others in yellow ($C336). Then MainMenuKeys. Keys 1-3 choose the controls and redraw the options, 4 redefines the keys and redraws the whole menu, 5 loads a saved game, 0 starts.
> Verified on the own instance: the menu reads 0..START GAME, 1..KEYBOARD (cyan), 2..KEMPSTON J/S, 3..INTERFACE 2, 4..REDEFINE KEYS, 5..LOAD SAVED GAME.
$C103 c MainMenuTitle ; The main menu without the clearing: the title, then the options
$C106 c MainMenuOptions ; The main menu's options (re-entered after keys 1-3)
$C109 t MenuStartText ; "0..START GAME" at (11,8) in double height, yellow
> PrintInline codes: attribute $46, setting $21 (double height), position row 11 column 8, the text, setting $00.
$C120 c MenuKeyboardOption ; Option 1, KEYBOARD, in the colour of Controls 1
$C128 t MenuKeyboardText ; Two new lines and "1..KEYBOARD"
$C137 c MenuKempstonOption ; Option 2, KEMPSTON J/S, in the colour of Controls 2
$C13F t MenuKempstonText ; Two new lines and "2..KEMPSTON J/S"
$C151 c MenuInterface2Option ; Option 3, INTERFACE 2, in the colour of Controls 3; then options 4 and 5
$C159 t MenuMoreText ; "3..INTERFACE 2", then in yellow "4..REDEFINE KEYS", "5..LOAD SAVED GAME"

$C192 c MainMenuKeys ; The main menu's keys: the title music until a key, then 0-5
> Waits until no key is held (each frame cycling the title's colours, $C820), plays the title music until a key ($C4A7), waits for a key (KEY-SCAN $028E, the character from the ROM's key table $0205): 0 returns Z, 1-3 set Controls and redraw the options, 4 RedefineKeys then the whole menu, 5 the load. Any other key is ignored.
$C192 ; wait until no key is held
$C19E ; the title music, until a key
$C1A1 MenuWaitKey
$C1AD ; the key's character from the ROM's main key table
$C1B5 ; 5: load a saved game
$C1B9 ; 0: start
$C1CB ; 4: redefine the keys, then the whole menu again
$C1D1 MenuStart ; Z: a new game
$C1D3 MenuKeyboard ; 1: keyboard
$C1D7 MenuKempston ; 2: Kempston
$C1DB MenuInterface2 ; 3: Interface 2
$C1DD MenuSetControls ; Controls, then the options again

$C1E3 c PrintTitle ; "NETHER" and "EARTH" in big white letters at the top
$C1E6 t TitleText ; "NETHER" at (1,10) and "EARTH" at (5,11), attribute $47, setting $32 (triple height, double width)
$C1FC c PrintTitleEnd ; PrintTitle's return; then the load option
$C1FD LoadGame ; key 5: the title again, PRESS PLAY ON TAPE
$C203 t PlayTapeText ; "PRESS PLAY ON TAPE" at (22,7), cyan, double height

$C21E c LoadGameBlocks ; Load a saved game: the checksum block, the game block; NZ when it is good
> Red border. Loads two headerless blocks (flag $00) through the ROM's LD-BYTES past its border set-up ($0562, the INC D / EX AF,AF' / DEC D / DI before it done here): 2 bytes to $5B00 (the checksum), then $24D1 bytes to $D92B-$FDFB. If SaveChecksum of the block matches, the first 45 bytes go back to $D7D3 (the 5 records of 9 bytes $B0E3 walks) and the next 168 to Buildings; returns NZ (MainMenu's caller skips the new game set-up). A mismatch prints LOADING ERROR!, beeps, waits for a key and restarts (Start).
> Not run in any recording; read from the code and the ROM's entry points. The block holds the robots ($DA00), the map ($DD00-$FCFF) and the variables $FD00-$FDFB (resources, clock, bases), so a load restores the whole game.
$C21E ; red border while it loads
$C222 ; 2 bytes: the checksum
$C229 ; flag $00, carry set: load
$C232 ; the game block
$C245 ; DE = the block's sum
$C248 ; the saved sum
$C252 ; good: the staged tables back to their places
$C255 ; the 5 records of 9 bytes at $D7D3
$C25D ; the buildings
$C266 ; NZ: a game was loaded
$C269 LoadBad ; LOADING ERROR!
$C26C t LoadErrorText ; "LOADING ERROR!" at (22,2), white, double size
$C282 c LoadError ; After LOADING ERROR!: a beep, a key, then the start again (Start)

$C28D c SaveGame ; Save the game to tape: the prompt, a key, the checksum block and the game block
> Called from the game loop when the SAVE key (Input bit 5, KeyTable record 2) is held ($A71A); the loop then redraws the screen and returns to play ($A68E). Installs StartInterrupt as the handler, prints PRESS RECORD AND PLAY / THEN ANY KEY at row 22, waits for a key, copies the 45 bytes at $D7D3 and Buildings to $D92B-$D9FF so that $D92B-$FDFB is one block, sums it into $5B00 and saves the 2 bytes and the block (flag $00) through the ROM's SA-BYTES past its pilot choice ($04D0) with a short pilot (HL = $0900).
> Not run in any recording; read from the code and the ROM's entry points. LoadGameBlocks reads the same two blocks.
$C28D ; the start-up's handler: the play handler's work stops
$C296 t RecordText ; "PRESS RECORD AND PLAY ", a new line, "     THEN ANY KEY     " at (22,0), cyan
$C2C9 c SaveGameBlocks ; After the prompt: a key, the staging, the checksum, the two blocks
$C2CC ; the radar map is rebuilt afterwards
$C2D1 ; stage the 5 records of 9 bytes at $D7D3 ...
$C2DC ; ... and the buildings at $D958
$C2E5 ; the checksum
$C2EC ; a short pilot
$C2EF ; the 2 checksum bytes, flag $00
$C2FB ; the game block $D92B-$FDFB

$C30F c SaveChecksum ; DE = the 16-bit sum of the $24D1 bytes at $D92B (the saved game block)
$C318 ChecksumLoop

$C325 c WaitAnyKey ; Wait until no key is held, then until one is (all half rows of port $FE)
$C32D WaitAnyKeyDown

$C336 c ControlsOptionAttr ; The attribute for menu option C: cyan ($45) when it is the current Controls, else yellow ($46)
$C33F b OptionAttrPlain 3 ; PrintInline: attribute $46
$C342 c ControlsOptionPlain ; ControlsOptionAttr's return, the option not chosen
$C343 ControlsOptionMarked ; the option chosen
$C346 b OptionAttrChosen 3 ; PrintInline: attribute $45
$C349 c ControlsOptionChosen ; ControlsOptionAttr's return, the option chosen

# --- redefine keys
$C34A c RedefineKeys ; Redefine the eight keys: UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, FIRE, PAUSE, ABORT, SAVE
> Key 4 of the main menu. Clears the screen, prints REDEFINE KEYS, waits until no key is held, clears KeyTable and asks for each key in turn: PrintKeyPrompt prints "PRESS KEY FOR " and the name that follows the CALL, DefineKey waits for a key not yet taken and fills the KeyTable record whose third byte HL points at (the port's high byte, the bit mask, the key code) and prints the key. KeyTable's records 0-7 are PAUSE, ABORT, SAVE, FIRE, UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT: Input bits 7 to 0. Waits for the last key's release and about 0.5 s, then back to the menu.
> Verified on the own instance: keys Q A O P M H B S gave KeyTable bf 10 48 / 7f 10 42 / fd 02 53 / 7f 04 4d / fb 01 51 / fd 01 41 / df 02 4f / df 01 50, the screen showed PRESS KEY FOR UP .. PAUSE with each key's letter, and the menu came back.
$C350 t RedefineTitleText ; "REDEFINE KEYS" at (2,9), white, double height, then position (5,3) and normal size
$C36A c RedefineKeysBody ; Wait for no key, clear KeyTable, ask for UP
$C36A ; wait until no key is held
$C372 ; KeyTable: 24 bytes of 0
$C37F ; "PRESS KEY FOR" and the name below
$C382 t KeyNameUp ; "   UP"
$C388 c ReadUpKey ; The key for UP (KeyTable record 4), then ask for DOWN
$C391 t KeyNameDown ; " DOWN"
$C397 c ReadDownKey ; The key for DOWN (record 5), then ask for LEFT
$C3A0 t KeyNameLeft ; " LEFT"
$C3A6 c ReadLeftKey ; The key for LEFT (record 6), then ask for RIGHT
$C3AF t KeyNameRight ; "RIGHT"
$C3B5 c ReadRightKey ; The key for RIGHT (record 7), then ask for FIRE
$C3BE t KeyNameFire ; " FIRE"
$C3C4 c ReadFireKey ; The key for FIRE (record 3), a blank line, then ask for PAUSE
$C3D0 t KeyNamePause ; "PAUSE"
$C3D6 c ReadPauseKey ; The key for PAUSE (record 0), then ask for ABORT
$C3DF t KeyNameAbort ; "ABORT"
$C3E5 c ReadAbortKey ; The key for ABORT (record 1), then ask for SAVE
$C3EE t KeyNameSave ; " SAVE"
$C3F4 c ReadSaveKey ; The key for SAVE (record 2), wait for its release and a pause, back to the menu
$C3FA RedefineWaitRelease ; until the SAVE key is let go


# ---- r4 ----
# r4: $C400-$CBFF, the redefine-keys helpers, the menu music, the title colour cycle, the construction menu.
# Evidence: code reading, work/play + work/deep L1, experiments on an own instance (port base 21800): redefine keys
# (key 4), the whole menu tune (5300 frames, analysis facts), part toggles in the construction menu with poked resources.

# --- the tail of the redefine-keys screen (the routine starts at $C34A, outside this range)
$C402 ; all keys up: a fixed pause of 65536 turns of the loop below (about 2 million T, 0.56 s) before the menu
$C405 RedefineKeysPause ; count BC down from $0000 through all 65536 values
$C406 RedefineKeysPauseNop ; a pad (the label is a false entry of the static walk: the string bytes before it read as JR NZ,$C406)
$C40B ; back to the caller of $C34A, which restarts the main menu ($C1CE)

$C40C c PrintKeyPrompt ; "PRESS KEY FOR " and then the key's name that follows the caller's CALL
> Called by the redefine-keys screen ($C37F, $C38E, ... $C3EB) once for each of the eight keys, with the key's name (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, FIRE, PAUSE, ABORT, SAVE) as an inline string after the CALL. It prints its own inline text (two new lines, attribute $46, "PRESS KEY FOR ", attribute $45), then jumps (not calls) to PrintInline at $C424: the return address on the stack is then the caller's, so PrintInline prints the caller's name string and returns past it. One prompt takes two screen rows.
$C40C ; our own text first
$C40F t PressKeyForText ; PrintInline text: new line, new line, attribute $46, "PRESS KEY FOR ", attribute $45
$C424 c PrintKeyPromptName ; print the caller's inline key name and return past it
> The tail of PrintKeyPrompt: a JP to PrintInline with the caller's return address on top of the stack, so the bytes after the caller's CALL $C40C (the name of the key to press) are printed and skipped.
$C424 ; the caller's string, and back past it

$C427 c DefineKey ; wait for a free key, store its port, mask and code into a KeyTable entry, print its name
> HL = the code byte (the third byte) of a KeyTable entry; the redefine screen passes the entries in the order UP (+$0E), DOWN (+$11), LEFT (+$14), RIGHT (+$17), FIRE (+$0B), PAUSE (+$02), ABORT (+$05), SAVE (+$08), so the entries map to the Input bits 3, 2, 1, 0, 4, 7, 6, 5 (ReadControls shifts entry 0 into bit 7). The screen cleared the whole table before the first key, so only keys already defined in this pass count as taken.
> It prints two spaces and sets attribute $46, then waits with the ROM KEY-SCAN ($028E) for a key; the key value E (0-39, row index 0-7 in bits 0-2, bit position from bit 4 down in bits 3-5) is turned into a code through the ROM key table $0205 (CAPS SHIFT gives $E3, the first byte past the table). A key whose code is already in one of the eight entries is refused and the wait goes on. Then it writes the code, the bit mask $20 >> (E/8 + 1) and the port high byte ($7F for row 0 down to $FE for row 7) backwards into the entry, and prints the key's name: SYM SH, ENTER, CAPS SH, SPACE, or the code as a character.
> Experiment: key 4 at the menu, then Q, Q, A, O, P, SPACE, ENTER, CAPS SHIFT, SYMBOL SHIFT left KeyTable = FB 01 51 / FD 01 41 / DF 02 4F / DF 01 50 for UP / DOWN / LEFT / RIGHT, 7F 01 20 for FIRE, BF 01 0D, FE 01 E3, 7F 02 0E for PAUSE / ABORT / SAVE; the second Q was refused.
$C427 ; keep the entry's address
$C428 ; two spaces, then the attribute of the name
$C42B t DefineKeyBlank ; PrintInline text: two spaces, attribute $46
$C430 c DefineKeyScan ; wait for a key that no entry holds yet
> Continues DefineKey after its inline text. On exit the entry is filled and the name printed (the RET is the one after each name string, or the one in PrintCharKeep's path for a plain character).
$C430 DefineKeyWait ; ROM KEY-SCAN: E = the key value, $FF none
$C435 ; no key yet
$C438 ; the ROM's key table: E -> the key's code
$C43E ; D = the code
$C43F ; the code bytes of the eight entries
$C444 KeyTakenLoop ; a code already used is refused: wait again
$C44C ; HL = the entry's code byte
$C44D ; store the code
$C44E ; C = the row index (0 = port $7FFE ... 7 = port $FEFE)
$C452 ; E / 8 = the key's place in its half-row, counted from bit 4
$C45A ; the mask: $20 rotated right E/8 + 1 times
$C45C KeyMaskShift
$C45F ; the entry's mask byte
$C461 ; the port high byte: $FF shifted right row + 1 times with 0 coming in
$C465 KeyPortShift
$C468 ; the entry's port byte
$C46A ; the code decides the name
$C46B ; SYMBOL SHIFT
$C46F ; its name, and the RET after the string
$C472 t KeyNameSymShift ; PrintInline text: "SYM SH"
$C479 c KeyNameSymShiftEnd ; a RET ($C9): PrintInline returns here and the routine ends
$C47A c CheckEnterKey ; DefineKey: is the new key ENTER?
> Part of DefineKey ($C427): code $0D prints "ENTER" and returns through the RET placed after the string.
$C47A ; ENTER
$C47E ; its name, and the RET after the string
$C481 t KeyNameEnter ; PrintInline text: "ENTER"
$C487 c KeyNameEnterEnd ; a RET ($C9) after the string
$C488 c CheckCapsShiftKey ; DefineKey: is the new key CAPS SHIFT?
> Part of DefineKey ($C427): code $E3 (the byte after the ROM's key table, what CAPS SHIFT alone looks up) prints "CAPS SH".
$C488 ; CAPS SHIFT
$C48C ; its name, and the RET after the string
$C48F t KeyNameCapsShift ; PrintInline text: "CAPS SH"
$C497 c KeyNameCapsShiftEnd ; a RET ($C9) after the string
$C498 c CheckSpaceKey ; DefineKey: SPACE prints its name, any other key prints its own character
> Part of DefineKey ($C427). A code other than $20 goes to PrintCharKeep (print the character A) and returns from there.
$C498 ; SPACE
$C49A ; any other key: print its code as a character and return
$C49D ; "SPACE"
$C4A0 t KeyNameSpace ; PrintInline text: "SPACE"
$C4A6 c DefineKeyDone ; RET: the end of DefineKey; also the target of the music's command 0
> The RET after the SPACE name. The two command tables of the menu music ($C553, $C572) point their command 0 here; command 0 never occurs in the tune, and it would not work: the dispatch leaves the voice pointer pushed, so this RET would return into the tune data.

# --- the menu music: two square-wave voices in a busy loop, a drum and the note reader in the interrupt
$C4A7 c MenuMusic ; play the menu tune until a number key is pressed
> Called once by the main menu ($C19E) after all keys are up. Saves SP in $FD08, installs MenuInterrupt as Handler and sets the music state: the bass voice reads the track $C678 through HL' (frames left C'), the lead voice the track $C702 through DE' (frames left B'), the drum the track $C665 through HL (frames left C); C' = B' = C = 1, so the first interrupt reads the first event of each. Then it runs the tone loop for ever: the main set's D and E count each voice's period down.
> The tone loop never returns: MenuInterrupt ends it by loading SP from $FD08 when a number key is down, so the RET of the interrupt returns from MenuMusic to the menu ($C1A1), which then reads the key. Ran 144 frames in work/play (12666-12814). The tune is 5120 frames (102.4 s) long in each voice and loops (experiment: both voices jumped back at frame 5099 of a menu left alone); docs: r4.md.
$C4A7 ; no interrupt while the state is set
$C4A8 ; the stack for the exit from MenuInterrupt
$C4B2 ; HL' = the bass track
$C4B5 ; DE' = the lead track
$C4B8 ; B' = C' = 1: read both voices at the first interrupt
$C4BC ; HL = the drum track, C = 1: read it at the first interrupt
$C4C2 ; start on an interrupt
$C4C3 c BassIdlePad ; tone loop: the time of a bass step without an edge
> The tone loop runs LeadStep and BassStep in turn, about 122 T a turn. A step whose counter has not run out takes this pad (PUSH AF, read Input and write it back unchanged, POP AF: 61 T with the DEC / JP) so both paths take about the same time, the pitch stays steady whatever the other voice does.
$C4C3 ; a pad: Input is written back unchanged, only the time counts
$C4CB c LeadStep ; tone loop: count the lead period down, at 0 a pulse
> D counts the lead voice's period in loop turns. At 0 it reloads D with the note's period (the operand $C4D0), sets the beeper (EAR and MIC, $18, or 0 when the voice is off: the operand $C4D2) for a pulse of 14 * width T (the operand $C4D6 = period / 4, at most 63), then clears it. The voice's pitch is about 3.5 MHz / (122 + 3.5) / period: period $7C about 225 Hz, $29 about 680 Hz.
$C4CB ; the lead counter
$C4CF ; reload: the note's period, written by LeadNextEvent ($C540)
$C4D1 ; $18 voice on, 0 off: written by LeadOn / LeadOff
$C4D3 ; the pulse's leading edge
$C4D5 ; the pulse width: period / 4, written by $C547
$C4D7 LeadPulse ; hold the pulse
$C4DB ; A = 0: the trailing edge
$C4E0 LeadIdlePad ; no edge: the same pad as BassIdlePad
$C4E4 c LeadIdlePadTail ; the rest of the lead pad (an entry only because work/deep starts its recording here)
> Write Input back and POP AF, then fall into BassStep. The recording of work/deep began inside the tone loop at this instruction, so its L1 lists the address as a root.
$C4E8 c BassStep ; tone loop: count the bass period down, at 0 a pulse
> The bass voice's twin of LeadStep: E is its counter, the operands $C4ED (period), $C4EF (on / off) and $C4F3 (pulse width) are written by BassNextEvent, BassOn and BassOff.
$C4E8 ; the bass counter
$C4EC ; reload: the note's period, written by BassNextEvent ($C52D)
$C4EE ; $18 voice on, 0 off: written by BassOn / BassOff
$C4F2 ; the pulse width: period / 4, written by $C534
$C4F4 BassPulse ; hold the pulse
$C4FA ; next turn: the lead voice

# MenuInterrupt ($C4FD, in game.ann): inner points
$C4FE ; the drum's frames run out: play the next drum record (inside the interrupt, the tone loop waits)
$C502 ; the voices' registers
$C503 ; the bass note's frames run out: the next bass event
$C507 ; the lead note's frames run out: the next lead event
$C50B ; the title's colours
$C50E ; half-rows 1-5 and 6-0: a number key, any menu choice
$C517 ; none: back to the tone loop
$C51B MenuMusicExit ; a number key: drop the tone loop, return from MenuMusic to the menu
$C51C ; SP as MenuMusic left it: the next RET returns to $C1A1

$C528 c BassNextEvent ; read the bass voice's next event: a note, or a command
> HL' = the bass track position. A byte of 9 or more is a note: the byte is the period (written into $C4ED, and period / 4 into the pulse width $C4F3) and the next byte its length in frames (C'). A byte below 9 is a command (BassCommand). Called by MenuInterrupt when C' runs out, and jumped to again by every command handler, so commands take no time.
$C528 ; the event byte
$C52B ; below 9: a command
$C52D ; the period into LD E,n
$C530 ; the pulse width: period / 4
$C537 ; C' = the note's frames
$C53B c LeadNextEvent ; read the lead voice's next event: a note, or a command
> The lead twin of BassNextEvent: DE' = the position, the note goes into $C4D0 / $C4D6 and its frames into B'.
$C53B ; the event byte
$C53E ; below 9: a command
$C540 ; the period into LD D,n
$C543 ; the pulse width: period / 4
$C54B ; B' = the note's frames
$C54F BassCommand ; command A of the bass track: jump to entry A of the table after the CALL
$C550 ; MusicDispatch pops the table address
$C553 BassCommandTable ; entry 0: JP $C4A6 (never used, it would RET into the pushed HL)
$C556 b BassCmd1 ; bass command 1: JP BassGoto ($C5A7), the 3-byte entry 1
$C559 c BassCmd2 ; bass command 2 (entry): JP BassCall
> A table entry of BassCommandTable: command 2 calls a pattern.
$C55C c BassCmd3 ; bass command 3 (entry): JP SetDrumTrack
> A table entry: command 3 starts another drum track.
$C55F c BassCmd4 ; bass command 4 (entry): JP BassOn
> A table entry: command 4 turns the bass voice on.
$C562 c BassCmd5 ; bass command 5 (entry): JP BassOff
> A table entry: command 5 turns the bass voice off (its notes keep their time, silent).
$C565 c BassCmd6 ; bass command 6 (entry): JP BassReturn
> A table entry: command 6 ends a pattern.
$C568 b BassCmd7 3 ; bass commands 7 and 8: JP SetDrumTrack twice (unused, the same as command 3)
$C56E c LeadCommand ; command A of the lead track: jump to entry A of the table after the CALL
> The lead twin of BassCommand. HL' (the bass position) is pushed so the handlers can pop it back.
$C56F ; MusicDispatch pops the table address
$C572 LeadCommandTable ; entry 0: JP $C4A6 (never used)
$C575 b LeadCmd1 ; lead command 1: JP LeadGoto ($C5B0)
$C578 c LeadCmd2 ; lead command 2 (entry): JP LeadCall
> A table entry of LeadCommandTable: command 2 calls a pattern.
$C57B b LeadCmd3 ; lead command 3: JP SetDrumTrack, a bass-only handler (it reads HL', the bass track): unusable from the lead track and unused
$C57E c LeadCmd4 ; lead command 4 (entry): JP LeadOn
> A table entry: command 4 turns the lead voice on.
$C581 c LeadCmd5 ; lead command 5 (entry): JP LeadOff
> A table entry: command 5 turns the lead voice off.
$C584 c LeadCmd6 ; lead command 6 (entry): JP LeadReturn
> A table entry: command 6 ends a pattern.
$C587 b LeadCmd7 3 ; lead commands 7 and 8: JP SetDrumTrack twice (unused)
$C58D c BassCall ; bass command 2 aa: play the pattern at aa, then go on after the command
> One level only: the return position (the address operand's high byte) is kept in $FD54; BassReturn resumes one byte past it.
$C58D ; HL' = the bass position again
$C591 ; the return point
$C594 ; HL' = the pattern
$C599 c LeadCall ; lead command 2 aa: play the pattern at aa, then go on after the command
> The return position is kept in $FD56.
$C599 ; HL' back (the bass position); DE' is the lead position
$C59E ; the return point
$C5A3 ; DE' = the pattern
$C5A7 c BassGoto ; bass command 1 aa: continue the track at aa (code: POP HL, read the word, JP BassNextEvent)
> Code that runs when the tune loops (frame 5099 of a menu left alone): the recordings never stayed in the menu that long, so the cut keeps it as data. POP HL; INC HL; LD A,(HL); INC HL; LD H,(HL); LD L,A; JP $C528. The bass track ends with 1 $C678.
$C5B0 c LeadGoto ; lead command 1 aa: continue the track at aa (code, run only when the tune loops)
> POP HL; EX DE,HL; INC HL; LD A,(HL); INC HL; LD H,(HL); LD L,A; EX DE,HL; JP $C53B. The lead track ends with 1 $C702.
$C5BB c BassReturn ; bass command 6: back from the pattern to the bass track
$C5BC ; one past the call's address operand
$C5C3 c LeadReturn ; lead command 6: back from the pattern to the lead track
$C5C4 ; one past the call's address operand
$C5CC c SetDrumTrack ; bass command 3 aa: the drum plays the track at aa from its next record
> Writes aa into the main set's HL, the drum position (the frames left of the current drum record are kept). Read through the bass voice's HL', so only the bass track may use it.
$C5D0 ; low byte into the drum's HL
$C5D5 ; high byte into the drum's HL

$C5DB c DrumStep ; play the next drum record (a short noise or sweep inside the interrupt)
> HL = the drum position, called by MenuInterrupt when the drum's frames (C) run out. A record is the frames until the next one and a kind: 0 a hiss (96 bytes of ROM from $08xx sent to the beeper, about 1 ms), 2 p a falling tone (48 cycles: the low half starts at p - 1 and shortens by 1, the high half starts at p / 2 and lengthens by 4), any other kind a rasp (80 ROM bytes from $00xx with a gap that mostly grows). The byte 1 in place of the frames is a goto: 1 aa. The tone loop stands still while a drum plays. In the 5300-frame experiment kinds 1 and 2 ran 72 times each.
$C5DB ; the frames, or 1
$C5DD ; 1 aa: goto
$C5E1 ; C = the frames to the next record
$C5E2 ; the kind
$C5EB DrumRasp ; any other kind: 80 ROM bytes $00xx; the gap (~B & $3F) grows $2F-$3F, once 256, then 1-$3E
$C5F0 DrumRaspLoop ; a ROM byte's EAR / MIC bits to the beeper
$C5F9 DrumRaspGap
$C601 DrumGoto ; the record 1 aa: continue at aa
$C608 DrumHiss ; kind 0: 96 ROM bytes $08xx straight to the beeper
$C60D DrumHissLoop
$C616 DrumSweep ; kind 2 p: the falling tone
$C61B ; the low half
$C61C ; the high half: p / 2
$C61E DrumSweepLoop ; the low half, one shorter each cycle
$C623 DrumSweepLow
$C626 ; the high half, 4 longer each cycle
$C62E DrumSweepHigh
$C635 c BassOn ; bass command 4: the bass pulses sound again
$C636 ; EAR and MIC into the bass pulse ($C4EF)
$C63F c BassOff ; bass command 5: the bass pulses go silent (the notes still take their time)
$C648 c LeadOn ; lead command 4: the lead pulses sound again
$C649 ; EAR and MIC into the lead pulse ($C4D2)
$C652 c LeadOff ; lead command 5: the lead pulses go silent
$C65B c MusicDispatch ; jump to entry A of the 3-byte JP table that follows the caller's CALL
> Called with A = the command (below 9) from BassCommand / LeadCommand; it pops its own return address (the table) and jumps to table + 3 * A, so the handler returns to nobody: each jumps back to the voice's reader.
$C65E ; the table, after the CALL
$C664 MusicDispatchJump ; to the command's JP

$C665 b DrumTrackSteady 5 ; drum track: a hiss every 32 frames (32 0, then 1 $C665)
> The drum tracks: records of (frames, kind[, p]) and gotos 1 aa; DrumStep. This one plays at the start and at the end of each pass of the tune; docs r4.md.
$C66A b DrumTrackUnused 2 ; a hiss record (32 0) that no goto reaches
$C66C b DrumTrackBeat 3 ; drum track: rasp 16, hiss 8, hiss 8, sweep $30 for 32 frames (64 frames), loops
> Started by the bass track's command 3 $C66C after the four intro riffs.
$C678 b BassTrack 3 ; the bass track: commands of 3 bytes (drums, pattern calls, the goto back)
> 3 $C665, 4 x 2 $C6BD (the riff), 3 $C66C, 2 x 2 $C6CC, 8 x 2 $C6DD, 5 x 2 $C6CC, 3 $C665, 1 $C678: 5120 frames.
$C6BD b BassRiff 2 ; bass pattern: the 128-frame intro riff on $7C with rests, then $52
> Notes are (period, frames), commands 4 / 5 one byte: $7C 16, off, $7C 8, on, $7C 16, off, $7C 64, $52 24, on, return.
$C6CC b BassWalk 2 ; bass pattern: $7C $6E $68 $5D twice, 64 frames each (512 frames)
$C6DD b BassRun 2 ; bass pattern: $7C in eighths with rests, then the run $8B $93 $A5 $BA $A5 $93 $8B $7C $8B $93 $A5 (128 frames)
$C702 b LeadTrack 3 ; the lead track: pattern calls, two long notes, the goto back
> 4 x riff $C789, 4 x phrase $C7AA, off, $C7AA, on, 5 x $C7C5, then $C7D2 / $C7C5 three times in turns, $3E 32, $37 32, 2 x $C7AA, 4 x the scale $C7DF, 4 x $C789, 8 x $C7C5, 4 x $C789, 1 $C702: 5120 frames.
$C789 b LeadRiff 2 ; lead pattern: $37 with rests, then trills $29 / $2E, $37, $3E (128 frames)
$C7AA b LeadPhrase 2 ; lead pattern: the tune's phrase $29 $2E $31 $37 $3E $49 $3E $45 $2E $29 $2E $34 $2E (256 frames)
$C7C5 b LeadArpeggioA 2 ; lead pattern: $3E $52 $7C twice (64 frames)
$C7D2 b LeadArpeggioB 2 ; lead pattern: $45 $5D $8B twice (64 frames)
$C7DF b LeadScale 2 ; lead pattern: the 16-note scale $29 up to $BA (high to low) twice, 4 frames a note (128 frames)

$C820 c TitleColourCycle ; cycle the ink of the NETHER EARTH title, one colour every 2 frames
> Counts $FD33 0-15 each call; on even counts it paints the attributes of rows 1-7, columns 10-21 (the big title printed by $C1E3) with BRIGHT, black paper and ink count / 2, so the ink runs through all 8 colours every 16 frames. Called every frame by the main menu's HALT loops ($C193, $C1A2) and by MenuInterrupt while the tune plays (26 calls over frames 0-26 of work/deep; it writes $582A-$58F5).
$C822 ; row 1, column 10
$C825 ; the phase, 0-15
$C82E ; odd: nothing this frame
$C831 ; BRIGHT, ink = phase / 2
$C833 ; 12 columns, 7 rows
$C836 TitleColourRow
$C837 TitleColourCell
$C83B ; to the next row: 12 + 20
$C846 TitleColourDone

