$CA0F c ConstructionMenuLoop ; wait for a control; fire acts on the cursor's item, a direction moves the cursor
> WaitKey until one of Input bits 0-4. Without fire: MoveMenuCursor ($CB00). With fire: column 0 EXIT MENU ($CB8E), column 1 START ROBOT ($CB52), column 2 toggles the part under the cursor.
> Choosing a part: a part already chosen is taken back (RefundPart) and cleared. A chassis (bipod, tracks, anti-grav: rows 0-2) replaces the chassis already chosen, which is refunded first. A fourth weapon is refused: of cannon, missiles, phasers and nuclear at most three ($78 never all set). The cost is paid from the part's own resource (PartCosts + 8) and what it lacks from the general resource; if the general one cannot pay the rest the part is refused (a replaced chassis stays refunded: the robot is left without one). Accepted: beep $64; refused or taken back: beep $78. Then it waits for fire up and pauses 10 frames.
> Experiment (general 10, electronics 1, chassis 2): bipod 3 -> chassis 0, general 9; tracks 5 -> bipod refunded, chassis 0, general 7; cannon 2 -> general 5; tracks again -> taken back, chassis 2, general 8. With 99 of everything: missiles, phasers, then nuclear refused (MenuParts $38).
$CA0F ; a direction or fire
$CA16 ; L = the column, H = the part row
$CA19 ; no fire: a move
$CA21 ; column 0: EXIT MENU
$CA26 ; column 1: START ROBOT
$CA2A PartToggle ; E = the part, D = its bit
$CA30 PartBitLoop
$CA34 ; C = the parts chosen
$CA38 ; chosen already: take it back
$CA3B ; a chassis (rows 0-2) ...
$CA40 ; ... and a chassis already chosen: refund that one first
$CA45 ; E = the chosen chassis' row
$CA48 FindChosenChassis
$CA4C ; its resources back
$CA4F ; no chassis now
$CA57 CheckWeaponCount ; cannon + missiles + phasers + nuclear all four: refused
$CA60 ; B = the part's cost
$CA68 ; the resource it takes first
$CA76 ; pay from the part's own resource
$CA7B ; short by the rest: the general resource pays it
$CA82 ; not enough: refused
$CA88 ; the own resource is spent to 0
$CA89 PartPaid ; store what is left, choose the part
$CA8F ; the beep of a part accepted
$CA94 ; repaint the part pictures and the preview
$CA97 ; reprint the resources
$CA9A AcceptFireUp ; wait for fire up
$CAA4 PartTakeBack ; refund and clear the part
$CAAC PartRefused ; refused or taken back: the low beep
$CAB7 RefuseFireUp ; wait for fire up
$CAC1 c RefundPart ; give back the cost of part E to MenuResources, the overflow to the general resource
> E = the part row. The cost goes back to the part's own resource; whatever lifts it above its value when the menu opened (Resources, 7 bytes below MenuResources) is what the general resource paid, so it is returned to the general one instead. Exact because each own resource has only one part in the menu at a time (one chassis, one of each weapon, electronics). Called by ConstructionMenuLoop ($CA4C, $CAA4).
$CAC1 ; B = the part's cost
$CAC9 ; the resource it takes first
$CAD8 ; refund it there
$CADA ; B = the same resource when the menu opened
$CAE3 ; not above it: done
$CAE5 ; above: cap it, the rest goes back to general
$CAE8 ; the general resource
$CB00 c MoveMenuCursor ; right / left change the column (0-2), down / up the part row (0-7) in column 2
> HL = MenuCursor (L the column, H the row), Input as WaitKey left it. Right adds 1 to the column, left subtracts 1; out of 0-2 nothing moves. Only without right / left: in the parts column up adds 1 to the row (towards ELECTRONICS, up the screen), down subtracts 1; out of 0-7 nothing moves. A move repaints the old item in $45, the new one in $46 and clicks (beep $14); every press ends in the 10-frame pause (MenuPause).
$CB00 ; right / left?
$CB09 ; right: the next column
$CB0E CursorLeft ; left: the previous one
$CB13 CursorColumnCheck ; 0-2 only ($FF too is refused)
$CB1B CursorUpDown ; down / up, only in the parts column
$CB27 ; down: the row below (a lower part)
$CB2C CursorUp ; up: the row above
$CB31 CursorRowCheck ; 0-7 only
$CB36 CursorMoved ; the old item back to $45
$CB3D ; the new cursor in $46
$CB45 ; a click
$CB4A c MenuPause ; 10 frames, then back to ConstructionMenuLoop
> The pause after every press (moves too); a shorter press falls into it and is lost, WALKTHROUGH 6.
$CB4C MenuPauseLoop
$CB52 c StartRobot ; START ROBOT: needs a chassis and a weapon; spend the resources, place the robot at the warbase's door, leave
> Refused (back to the menu after the pause) without a chassis (MenuParts bits 0-2) or without a weapon (bits 3-6); electronics alone count as neither. Otherwise MenuResources become Resources, the free slot becomes a robot of ours (+$0A = 0) at column ShipX, row ShipY + 4 (PlaceRobot writes +0 to +4 and marks the map cell), +7 = MenuParts, and a rising chirp plays (Beep with $50 down to $14 by 5). Then LeaveConstructionMenu. In work/deep the robot built at frame 356 sits at (22, 9): the pad at (22, 5) plus 4 rows.
$CB55 ; no chassis: refused
$CB5A ; no weapon: refused
$CB5F ; the menu's spending becomes real
$CB6A ; the robot stands 4 rows below the ship (the pad)
$CB73 ; not in the menu, not under direct control
$CB77 ; place it on the map
$CB7A ; its parts
$CB80 ; the chirp: $50 down to $14, a shorter period each beep
$CB82 BuildChirp
$CB8E LeaveConstructionMenu ; EXIT MENU and the end of START ROBOT: back to play
$CB8E ; a long low beep
$CB93 ; $FD30 = 5: the ship climbs 5 game turns on its own, so it does not sink straight back into the menu
$CB98 ; the play screen redrawn
$CBA2 ; the play interrupt again
$CBA9 c PaintMenuCursor ; paint the item under MenuCursor with attribute C ($46 the cursor, $45 the rest)
> Column 0 / 1: two rows of 5 cells, EXIT MENU at rows 21-22 columns 4-8, START ROBOT columns 10-14. Column 2: the part's name, 11 cells from column 21 of row 22 - 3 * row (BIPOD row 22 up to ELECTRONICS row 1). Called by ConstructionMenuSetup and MoveMenuCursor.
