> The font is at $6680 + 8*code ($6780 for the space, right after the view buffer). 8 lines into ($FD04), the attribute ($FD13) into ($FD14), both advanced.
$D4B7 ; the font
$D4C2 GlyphLineLoop
$D4DA c PrintBigChar ; Print character A scaled: each line repeated $FD16 times, width 1 or 2 bytes ($FD17)
> Width 1: each glyph line written $FD16 times (2: a character two rows high, "RADAR:"). Width 2: each glyph byte doubled to 16 bits. Then the screen address moves on by the width and a block of width x height attribute cells gets $FD13. 180 calls in the deep recording.
$D4E7 ; width 2: doubled pixels
$D4F1 TallLineLoop
$D4F5 TallRepeatLoop
$D502 WideChar
$D50C WideLineLoop
$D511 WideBitLoop ; each bit twice into DE
$D522 WideRepeatLoop
$D532 BigCharAttrs ; advance, then the attribute block
$D54A BigAttrRowLoop
$D54C BigAttrCellLoop
$D56A ; the frame counter: the play loop waits for 10 ($AE1D)
$D56E ; the player's radar picture, cyan
$D573 ; the other picture each frame
$D57D ; the enemy's radar picture, yellow
$D582 ; show it
$D585 ; a sound effect running?
$D58B ; counts up each frame; $80 ends a rising one, 0 a falling one
$D594 ; the flags of A: M noise, P the falling tone
$D59E c ShowRadar ; Show radar picture HL (16 lines x 16 bytes) at (22,6) in attribute C
> The interrupt alternates the two pictures $D800 (terrain + the player's robots, attribute $4D) and $D900 (terrain + the enemy's robots, $4E) every frame, so the terrain stays and each side's robots blink in their colour. The attributes are rows 22 and 23, columns 6-21.
$D5A1 ; screen line 0 of character row 22, column 6
$D5A4 RadarLineLoop
$D5B5 ; the attributes of row 22
$D5BB ; row 23
$D5BD c FillAttr16 ; Write C into 16 bytes from HL
$D5BF FillAttrLoop
$D5C4 c SoundEffect ; The interrupt's sound: A = $FD53 (1-$7F a falling tone, $FD-$FF noise)
> A positive: a tone of ~500 / (A+2) pulses with a delay of A between them, the levels taken from bit 4 of the ROM bytes from $0000 (a rough noise), so the pitch falls as A counts up each frame. $FD53 = 1 when a shot is fired ($B709). A negative: 30 random speaker levels (Random): the crackle of a hit. The shot code sets $FC (a miss: 3 frames), $FA (a hit: 5 frames) or $C8 (a robot destroyed: 55 frames). Seen in the deep recording at frames 18680-18712: 1, 2, ... $16, then $FA-$FF.
$D5C7 ; B = the delay between pulses
$D5CA ; C = about 500 / (A + 2) pulses
$D5D3 PulseCountLoop
$D5D8 ; the ROM as noise
$D5DB PulseLoop
$D5E2 PulseDelay
$D5E8 ; speaker off
$D5EC NoiseSound
$D5EE NoiseLoop
$D5F8 c BuildRadar ; Build both radar pictures for the 128 columns from $FD1C
> Each map cell of the window is one pixel: set when its block type is $0F or more (the tall blocks). Copies the terrain picture to $D900, then puts the dot of every robot in play (+$01 not 0, except the one under direct control, +$0A = 2) into its side's picture (ToggleRadarDot). Called by $B029 when the ship moves the window ($FD52); once in the deep recording, at frame 35.
$D603 ; 16 rows of 16 bytes
$D606 RadarRowLoop
$D608 RadarByteLoop
$D60B RadarBitLoop
$D60F ; carry: the type is $0F or more
$D61C ; the next map row
$D621 ; the same terrain for the enemy's picture
$D62C ; the robots
$D632 RadarRobotLoop
$D649 ; not the robot under direct control (the ship's dot stands for it)
$D65A c ToggleRadarDot ; XOR a 2x2 dot at map column HL, row C into radar picture B (0 player, 1 enemy)
> The dot covers (x, y), (x+1, y) and the line above. Nothing outside the window (RadarDotAddress returns past this routine). XOR: the same call removes the dot again, so the robot code toggles the old position before PlaceRobot toggles the new one.
$D65D ; C = the pixel mask
$D662 ; the next pixel, maybe in the next byte
$D66B ; the line above
$D67D c RadarDotAddress ; HL = the radar byte of column HL, row C in picture B, A = the pixel mask
> Column x - $FD1C must be 0-126, else it pops the return address and returns from ToggleRadarDot. Address = $D800 + $100*B + 16*y + (x-$FD1C)/8, mask = $80 >> ((x-$FD1C) AND 7).
$D68D ; $D800 or $D900
$D696 ; L = 16*y + x/8
$D6A0 ; the mask: $80 shifted right x AND 7 times
$D6A6 ; outside the window: return from the caller too


# ---- d1 ----
# d1: the data blocks $5B00-$A5FF and $D6A8-$FCE8 (view buffer, font, sprites, tables, radar, shots, robots, map).
# Images of every sprite: work/ann/d1-img/ (sprites-sheet.png, ADDR-NAME.png; black ink, yellow the background the
# mask keeps, white the pixels the mask clears). Findings and the proposed resources: work/ann/d1.md.

$5B00 b ViewBuffer 20 ; The view's back buffer: 160 lines of 20 bytes (160 x 160 pixels)
> The isometric view is composed here and copied to the screen at $4021 (character columns 1-20, rows 1-20) by CopyViewToScreen, an unrolled LDI x 20 a pixel line. Line N is at $5B00 + 20 * N, top line first. ClearViewBuffer clears it (198 x 8 PUSHes from $6780 down, then $5B00-$5B1F by LDIR; SP saved in $FD08), FillViewCorner lays the black triangle above the map's back edge (runs of $FF, 10 bytes on the top 8 lines, 2 fewer each 8 lines) and DrawViewBackground the diagonal edges from ViewEdgeA / ViewEdgeB; DrawSprite blits every sprite into it (SpriteByteLoop: AND mask, OR graphic, the rows upward, 20 bytes a line). Other uses of the same bytes: ShowRobotDesign draws the robot preview of ConstructionMenu here and copies 4 bytes x 72 lines from $6168 to the screen at $48E0; NuclearFlash (the nuclear blast's flash) saves the view's 20 x 20 attributes into $5B00-$5C8F and puts them back; the tape save / load SaveGameBlocks / LoadGameBlocks keep the 2-byte checksum of the saved block at $5B00 (its first tape block).
> In the image it is zero up to $6731; $6732-$677F hold 78 bytes of leftovers of the loading (ROM addresses, a BASIC stack copy), never read before the first clear. The font index (PrintChar, PrintBigChar: $6680 + code x 8) starts inside this buffer: codes below $20 would take their glyphs from its last 256 bytes; no text prints them.
$6780 b Font 8 ; Font: 64 glyphs of 8 bytes, codes $20-$5F
> Glyph = $6680 + code * 8, top line first, 1 = ink. Codes $20-$5F: space, punctuation, digits, "@" is the (c) sign, A-Z, then "[", "\", "]" and an up arrow at $5B-$5E, $5F blank. Read by PrintChar ($D4C2: 8 lines straight to the screen, one attribute from $FD13 to the cell at ($FD14)) and PrintBigChar ($D4F5: each line repeated $FD16 times; with $FD17 >= 2 ($D50C) each pixel doubled to 16 pixels wide). Every text of the game goes through them (PrintInline and the number printers). Image: d1-img/6780-Font.png.
$6980 b SprTracksX 2 ; Sprite 22 (even column; SpriteTable 44): tracks, moving along X (directions 0-1), 27 rows x 3 bytes
> The sprites run from here to $A569, 88 blocks, each reached through SpriteTable (sprite id x 2 + the column parity). A block: the height in pixel lines, the width in bytes, then the rows from the BOTTOM line up, each row width x (mask, graphic) byte pairs: screen = (screen AND mask) OR graphic (SpriteByteLoop into ViewBuffer, DrawMaskedUp straight to the screen). The parity is bit 0 of the half-byte column DrawSprite computes (2 x X + Y - 24): the odd entry is the same picture shifted right by 4 pixels (one byte wider where it needs to), except where noted: for map objects the column parity is the row parity, and map types 12, 13, 14, 17, 18 use the two entries for two different pictures.
> Drawn by DrawRobotPart through DrawSprite for every part of a robot in the view (sprite id = $16 + PartSpriteTable[part * 4 + direction]), stacked by PartHeights.
$6982 b SprTracksXRows 6 ; SprTracksX: 27 rows of 3 (mask, graphic) pairs, bottom line first
$6A24 b SprTracksXOdd 2 ; Sprite 22 (odd column; SpriteTable 45): tracks, moving along X (directions 0-1), 27 rows x 4 bytes
> Drawn by DrawRobotPart through DrawSprite for every part of a robot in the view (sprite id = $16 + PartSpriteTable[part * 4 + direction]), stacked by PartHeights. Also the part's icon in ConstructionMenu ($C86D, drawn straight to the screen by DrawMaskedUp).
$6A26 b SprTracksXOddRows 8 ; SprTracksXOdd: 27 rows of 4 (mask, graphic) pairs, bottom line first
$6AFE b SprTracksY 2 ; Sprite 23 (even column; SpriteTable 46): tracks, moving along Y (directions 2-3), 28 rows x 3 bytes
